    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920480": {
      "title": "Enrolment Advisor",
      "description": "Enrolment Advisor Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 27/10/2026 3 full-time vacancies - 2 x fixed term until December 2027 and 1 x continuing On campus Gym & childcare, Wellbeing program, excellent leave entitlements Join a rewarding, fast-paced team environment supporting student enrolments! Job Reference: 1694256 Want to make a real difference in students’ lives? Join Curtin University’s Enrolments team as an Enrolment Advisor, where you will deliver high-quality customer service that directly enhances the student experience. About the Role In this pivotal role, you will manage and maintain student enrolments and support curriculum structures using our cutting edge systems. From admission to completion, you will help enable student self-management across all courses offered at Curtin’s Bentley and Kalgoorlie campuses, as well as online programs through Open Universities Australia. You’ll be the key point of contact for resolving enrolment-related enquiries within agreed timeframes, while ensuring student and curriculum records are kept accurate and confidential. If you're approachable, solution-focused, and thrive in a dynamic environment, this is your opportunity to contribute meaningfully to our students’ success. Key Responsibilities: Deliver high-quality, customer-focused support by providing accurate, consistent, and timely advice on enrolment and curriculum matters. Accurately maintain student records and curriculum information in corporate systems, ensuring confidentiality and adherence to deadlines, timeframes, and approval processes. Build and maintain strong professional relationships with staff across Schools, Faculties, Central Teams, Open Universities Australia, and other external stakeholders. Manage and resolve student enquiries within agreed turnaround times in alignment with service KPIs. Maintain up-to-date knowledge of university systems used to support enrolment and curriculum services, such as Student One and AKARI. SELECTION CRITERIA Who we are looking for: Proven experience in delivering high-quality customer service, with a strong commitment to client satisfaction. Excellent organisational skills, with the ability to effectively manage time, set priorities, and meet deadlines in a dynamic environment. Strong problem-solving skills, using sound judgement and knowledge to determine appropriate solutions. Well-developed interpersonal skills, with the ability to build and maintain effective professional relationships across diverse teams and levels. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to clearly and professionally convey information and prepare accurate documentation. Demonstrated ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a team environment. Experience using corporate student information systems such as Student One (desirable). Role Requirements: As this is a fixed term role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. Student Services Student Services aim to enable Curtin’s vision through the delivery of a seamless student-centred experience accessible at any time, on any device, from any location. The area is responsible for the delivery of a suite of essential services, processes and systems which underpin and drive core Curtin University operations. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2026 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page: https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: 3 x Full-time vacancies - 2 x fixed term until December 2027 and 1 x continuing These full-time roles attract a remuneration of $85,781 - $93,836 (G05) per annum + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience, addressing the Selection Criteria noted above If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00PM (AWST), Tuesday, 10 November 2026 Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=921989": {
      "title": "Business Development Manager - Translation and Impact",
      "description": "Business Development Manager - Translation and Impact Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 31/10/2026 Fixed term role until 30 June 2028. Part-time or full-time (minimum 0.7 FTE); Translate specialist STEM capability into meaningful real-word opportunities Competitive remuneration, leave, super & salary packaging provisions Reference Number: 1701413 About the Team: Radio Astronomy applies ‘STEM’ based disciplines to capture and analyse ultra-faint signals from the universe. In doing this, we push the boundaries of technology and techniques in electromagnetics, signal processing, software development, and radio frequency communications. The Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy (CIRA) and The International Centre for Radio Astronomy Research (ICRAR) deliver a dedicated Translation and Impact program (T&I) with a strong reputation for forging meaningful, mutually beneficial collaborations with industry to share our skills, knowledge, and expertise for mutual benefit. By collaborating with experts in other domains, we can help boost capability, train WA’s future workforce, grow businesses, and create jobs. About the Role: You will work with the Director, Translation and Impact to identify, develop, manage, and provide broad support to the technical experts who deliver on collaborative projects. In this role you will develop a thorough appreciation of the capabilities and underlying skill sets of our team (including physics, electrical engineering, and data science), and matching them to opportunities for collaboration and impact outside of radio astronomy. You will engage with a variety of internal and external stakeholders, including State, Commonwealth, and international governments, various industries (commonly, Defence and Space), and other research organisations. This will involve identifying and leveraging funding opportunities to support ICRAR’s Translation and Impact objectives. You will be responsible for providing broad project support, including operational support, and navigating internal Curtin policies and procedures to enable our subject matter experts to deliver on commercial projects as required. This will involve budgeting, commercial contract negotiation, contract delivery, and reporting. Please note will consider applicants seeking a full time or part time position (minimum 0.7FTE), with some flexibility as to working hours (having regard to individual project or business requirements). Your responsibilities will include: Building relationships with, liaising with, and maintaining communication and productive relationships with relevant partners and stakeholders. Managing all activities related to assigned projects, from start up, planning, implementation and close-out, to achieve the defined project outcomes. This will include liaising with internal University service functions (e.g. Research Office, Risk & Compliance, International Risk and Security, People and Culture, Legal Services, Financial Services). Developing relevant business cases, funding proposals, and project initiation documentation, including work plans, timelines, costings, budgets, commercial contracts, and communication strategies. Identifying, source, manage and assign necessary project resources. Identifying impacts and risks associated with the project and reporting on and managing these. This may include field trip risk assessments, safety protocols and logistics. Preparing documents required for the export of certain technical goods and services, including Defence Export Controls and other customs requirements, ensuring compliance with Curtin University policies and procedures. Managing internal and external resources associated with the delivery of assigned projects, and managing the procurement of any third party services, ensuring relevant practices and processes are adhered to. Who We Are Looking For (Selection Criteria): We are looking for someone personable, with experience applying commercial and political acumen, servicing a combination of stakeholders across industry, government, and not for profit sectors. The scope of your role can vary significantly between individual projects, stakeholders, and activities. For that reason, you will be comfortable working in an environment that is more driven by specific project needs than by explicit rote tasks. In particular, you will be comfortable adapting rapidly to a diversity of tasks/roles, to prioritise workloads, and multitask, as opportunities and initiatives move through their lifecycle. You will be comfortable working alongside technical subject matter experts to understand and communicate effectively our industry-relevant capabilities. Previous experience in a relevant technical discipline (e.g. involving physical sciences, computing, or engineering) will enable you to understand and credibly communicate CIRA capabilities to stakeholders. Essential Criteria: Relevant tertiary qualifications and extensive experience in business development and industry engagement within a complex organisation; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education training. Demonstrated commercial acumen and a successful track record in securing and integrating funding support from industry, government and other organisations into collaborative ventures. Demonstrated experience in contract negotiation and management. Demonstrated ability to engage and influence others and a proven capacity to build and maintain effective and productive relationships with a wide range of internal and external stakeholders. Demonstrated high level communication (written and oral) with the ability to engage and present information clearly and informatively to diverse audiences and prepare business cases. Demonstrated experience in conducting analytical research and providing reports on public documents to identify significant information. Desirable: Familiarity with commercial contract terms and conditions generally, and ideally (but not necessarily) the negotiation of commercial contract terms or contract management in any sector will be highly valued. Ideally (but not necessarily) some experience supporting demand-driven (commercial) research or working in a university environment. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction . Your new Team – CIRA: The Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy (CIRA) is a multi-disciplinary research institute engaged in a range of projects involving or motivated by radio astronomy. CIRA’s activities draw together academic research and professional staff from a variety of science, engineering, and computing disciplines to tackle unique and demanding challenges in astronomy and non-astronomy domains. Committed to research translation and impact (T&I), CIRA is committed to engaging with partners to share our skills, knowledge, and expertise for mutual benefit and impact beyond astronomy. CIRA is the lead organisation and operator of the Murchison Widefield Array (MWA), a key participant in the international SKA Project. CIRA is also the Curtin University node of the International Centre for Radio Astronomy Research (ICRAR) which was established in 2009, and one of the largest and most enduring academic research initiatives in Western Australia. CIRA celebrates diversity and is an equal opportunity employer. We encourage applications from all cultures, races, nationalities, genders, identities, and abilities aligning with the selection criteria, and are committed to preserving our principles of equitable and inclusive work and study environments. To learn more about CIRA and our projects and initiatives, please visit the below websites: www.astronomy.curtin.edu.au www.icrar.org About Curtin University We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This fixed term, full-time (or part-time at 0.7 FTE or higher), G08 level role attracts a full-time remuneration of $122,699 - $130,758 (pro rata for part-time) + 17% superannuation . Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest, experience and addressing the section criteria for the role (no more than two pages) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8pm AWST, Sunday, 15 November 2026 Disclaimer: Please note that there is no standardised time for the closing of adverts on the specified date. Therefore, we recommend submitting your application ahead of this deadline. Late applications will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=921888": {
      "title": "Systems Support Officer (s51)",
      "description": "Systems Support Officer (s51) Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 31/10/2026 Full-time opportunity until 31 December 2027 | Bentley Campus Support the OASIS Enhancement Program and help make everyday digital experiences Flexible working arrangements | 2 days per week from home Job Reference: 1684428 At Curtin we are committed to embedding the voices and perspectives of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples at the forefront of our decision-making to create an environment in which everyone thrives. In line with Curtin’s 2030 Strategic Plan, Curtin University is committed to optimising employment outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. In an effort to increase Aboriginal and Torres Strait representation in the University, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are strongly encouraged to apply for this role, pursuant to Section 51 of the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA). About the Role At Curtin University, we're transforming how students connect with the information, services and support they need throughout their university journey. Our OASIS Enhancement Program is an exciting initiative designed to create a more connected, intuitive and personalised digital experience for students. Building on the successful refresh of our student portal, the program will introduce new functionality to support student orientation, academic planning, communications and campus engagement. We're looking for a motivated and solutions-focused Systems Support Officer to join our Development and User Experience team and contribute to this important program of work. Key responsibilities include: Manage incoming enquiries and support tickets, keeping users informed and escalating complex matters. Support the Senior Business Analyst with workflow coordination and day-to-day systems administration. Assist with OASIS access, provide user guidance and training, and maintain clear process documentation. Work with developers, UX analysts and colleagues to resolve issues, capture feedback and support ongoing improvements. SELECTION CRITERIA We welcome applicants with transferable experience in administration, student services, service desks or systems support. To be successful, you will demonstrate: A relevant degree or equivalent combination of experience, education and training. Experience supporting users, administering processes or working with digital systems, with a willingness to learn. Strong organisation, problem-solving and attention to detail, including managing competing requests and knowing when to escalate. Clear, culturally respectful communication and a collaborative approach to supporting people with varied technical confidence. Experience with ticketing systems such as ServiceNow, access administration or user training is desirable, not essential. Role Requirements As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants will be subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2026 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page: https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This full-time, fixed-term role attracts a salary package of $85,781 - $93,836 (G05) per annum plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience, addressing the Selection Criteria noted above If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00PM (AWST), 13 November 2026 Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=921847": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Co-curricular Development",
      "description": "Coordinator, Co-curricular Development Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 31/10/2026 Full-time, continuing (permanent) opportunity | Curtin Perth (Bentley) Campus Commencing January 2027 | Help students build skills beyond the classroom Grow Curtin Extra and strengthen student engagement and employability outcomes Job Reference: 1701292 About the Role Curtin University is seeking a collaborative and creative Coordinator, Co-curricular Development to champion high-quality co-curricular participation and development across the University. You’ll work with students, staff and partners to strengthen the quality, reach and impact of co-curricular opportunities, with a particular focus on improving equitable participation for students from low socioeconomic status (LSES) backgrounds and First Nations students. Through this work, you’ll help students make the most of experiences that enrich their studies and strengthen their graduate outcomes. A key focus of the role is Curtin Extra, our framework for enhancing students’ co-curricular learning and recognising their participation and achievements. You’ll help students understand and articulate the skills they develop through these experiences, while supporting quality programs and effective governance across Curtin. Your responsibilities will include: Coordinate the growth, delivery, governance and evaluation of the Curtin Extra framework and support high-quality co-curricular programs across Curtin. Help students recognise and articulate the skills and capabilities they develop through co-curricular participation. Partner with university teams, students and external organisations to expand equitable access to high-quality co-curricular opportunities, particularly for underrepresented student groups. Build awareness of Curtin Extra through advocacy, presentations, training, resources and promotional initiatives. Support effective governance, data systems, participation records, compliance, evaluation and recognition processes. Use student engagement data and program evaluation to identify opportunities for improvement and strengthen student and graduate outcomes. SELECTION CRITERIA You are a relationship-focused program coordinator who enjoys bringing people together, developing new ideas and translating them into meaningful student experiences. You balance creativity with strong organisation and sound governance. To be successful, you will demonstrate: A relevant qualification with considerable relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of experience, education and training. Demonstrated experience managing complex projects and delivering effective programs or services. Ability to develop and implement processes and strategies that improve quality and outcomes. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, including the ability to influence and build cooperation across diverse stakeholder groups. Excellent organisational skills, with experience coordinating people and events, prioritising work and using initiative. Creative problem-solving skills and the ability to contribute effectively within a busy, collaborative and culturally diverse team. Confidence using multiple systems, CRM platforms and reporting tools. Competence in using databases, student management systems and digital platforms, together with an understanding of student engagement, equity and inclusion in higher education, would be highly regarded. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, applicants require permanent working rights in Australia to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a criminal record check and relevant background, integrity and reference checks in accordance with University requirements. The role may require work outside normal office hours. Your new Team: – Student Life and Community Based within Student Life and Community, you’ll contribute to Curtin’s student success and community development priorities. The role works closely with students, academic and professional colleagues, the Curtin Student Guild and industry and community partners to support co-curricular learning and recognition across Curtin’s campuses and partner institutions. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2026 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This full-time, continuing (permanent) role attracts a salary package of $109,277 - $117,331 (G07) per annum plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8.00PM (AWST), 13 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=921691": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching Focused) - Digital & Social Media",
      "description": "Lecturer (Teaching Focused) - Digital & Social Media Faculty of Humanities Perth, Western Australia Contract Part Time 31/10/2026 Fixed-term, Part time 0.5FTE contract Ending 14th of January 2028 Based on Bentley Campus WA Diverse & inclusive environment that fosters innovation, collaboration, & sust Reference: 1700894 About the Role: Support student learning as a teaching-focused Lecturer in digital and social media. In this part-time role (0.5 FTE), you will deliver engaging teaching, teaching both face-to-face and online, develop innovative learning materials and contribute to curriculum design and unit coordination. Your primary contribution will be to teaching and learning, drawing on current research, scholarship and professional practice to enrich the student experience. You will maintain your discipline knowledge, develop your teaching practice and scholarly activities, and build your profile in teaching, academic service and leadership within a collegial and supportive environment. Your responsibilities will include: Teaching and learning: Plan and deliver engaging online and face-to-face learning activities in digital and social media, drawing on current discipline knowledge and research-informed teaching practices. Curriculum and coordination: Collaborate with colleagues to design innovative curricula and learning resources, coordinate units, and undertake associated teaching administration. Assessment and feedback: Assess and moderate student work, provide constructive feedback and tailor learning support to help students achieve the intended learning outcomes. Student support and supervision: Support students’ transition to university and respond to diverse learning needs. Potentially supervise honours and postgraduate coursework students, including master’s-level research projects, and higher degree by research students as appropriate. Teaching scholarship and development: Maintain current discipline knowledge, incorporate research, scholarship and professional practice into teaching, and contribute to professional development and peer review to continually improve student learning. Service and engagement: Contribute to academic service, discipline and professional activities, and community engagement, while fostering a supportive working environment and modelling professional conduct consistent with the University’s Code of Conduct and values. Who We Are Looking For/Selection Criteria: We are seeking an educator with expertise in digital and social media, a commitment to high-quality, inclusive teaching and a collaborative approach to academic life. To be successful in this role, you will have: A doctoral qualification in digital and social media or a relevant discipline. Demonstrated high-level teaching skills and a reflective approach to teaching delivery, including online teaching delivery. A strong understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice relevant to the discipline. An emerging record of contribution to the scholarship of teaching. Demonstrated experience in, or capacity to undertake, unit coordination. High-level communication, interpersonal and organisational skills, a commitment to a collegial and supportive working environment, and the ability to engage with students and staff with cross-cultural sensitivity. A demonstrated commitment to applying relevant policies, procedures and legislation in day-to-day work. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role This is a part-time role (0.5 FTE). Working days will be discussed at interview. Please indicate your preferred working days in your cover letter, including whether these days are flexible. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Part-Time 0.5 Fixed Term ALB level role attracts a remuneration of $121,857 - $144,352 pro rata to 0.5 FTE + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages, OR A document addressing the selection criteria for the role If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Thursday 12th of November 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
