    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920845": {
      "title": "FinOps Lead",
      "description": "FinOps Lead Office of Corporate Services Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 28/10/2026 Full time, continuing (permanent) position Generous leave entitlements and 17% superannuation Work-life balance options, inc. potential flexible work-from-home arrangements Job Reference: 1700919 About the Role We are looking for an experienced and commercially minded FinOps leader to play a key role in shaping Curtin University’s cloud financial management capability. This is an exciting opportunity for someone with strong technical insight, sound financial judgement, and the confidence to influence change in a complex digital environment. Working within our Digital Platforms Team , you will lead cloud financial operations and help ensure Curtin gains maximum value from its cloud investments through strong governance, transparency, accountability and continuous improvement. As a trusted leader in Curtin’s cloud journey, you will champion a cost-conscious culture across the university, partnering with technical and business stakeholders to embed value, efficiency and financial responsibility into the way digital platforms and services are designed, delivered and operated. This role brings together cloud expertise, financial acumen, stakeholder engagement and people leadership, offering the chance to make a visible impact as Curtin continues to mature its digital strategy and cloud capability. Key Responsibilities: Lead and mentor a FinOps team to deliver strategic cloud financial management outcomes and uplift FinOps capability across the university. Develop and implement strategies to optimise cloud costs, improve value from cloud investments and support financially sustainable cloud consumption. Provide clear visibility and insight into cloud spend across compute, storage, applications and broader cloud services. Develop and enforce cost allocation models, tagging standards, and chargeback/showback frameworks to improve financial transparency, governance and accountability. Leverage data insights to analyse spend trends, identify optimisation opportunities and provide informed recommendations to executive stakeholders. Collaborate with technology leaders and business stakeholders to promote efficient cloud utilisation, support practical change and strengthen financially responsible practices. Lead education, engagement and continuous improvement initiatives to champion FinOps principles, build cloud financial literacy and mature cloud financial management tools, reporting platforms and cost monitoring capabilities. Selection Criteria: We are seeking an experienced leader with strong financial operations expertise, the ability to influence at a strategic level, and a solid understanding of enterprise cloud environments. You will bring: Demonstrated experience in FinOps, cloud financial management, technology finance, or cloud cost optimisation within a complex enterprise environment. Strong commercial and financial acumen, including budgeting, forecasting, financial reporting, and cost optimisation practices. Working knowledge of cloud platforms such as AWS and/or Microsoft Azure, including cloud billing, consumption, and optimisation principles. Experience implementing or managing FinOps practices such as chargeback/showback models, cloud spend reporting, and cost governance frameworks. Excellent stakeholder engagement and communication skills, with the ability to influence and educate both technical and non-technical audiences. Proven leadership capability, with experience driving strategic initiatives, leading teams, and building collaborative relationships across an organisation. A proactive and strategic mindset, with the ability to identify opportunities, drive continuous improvement, and shape organisational direction. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants will be subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Your new Team: Digital, Technology & Solutions, Platforms The Platforms Team is the engine room of Curtin University’s digital ecosystem. We design, build and operate the core technology platforms that underpin the entire university - from cloud infrastructure and automation to data, integration, and foundational digital services. Our work enables every faculty, researcher, student and staff member to innovate, connect and succeed. This is a unique opportunity to shape and mature FinOps capability within one of Australia’s leading universities. You will have the opportunity to influence enterprise-wide cloud financial strategy, drive meaningful operational efficiencies, build a community of practice, and help embed a culture of financial accountability and cloud optimisation across the university. The role offers a rare combination of strategic influence, leadership responsibility, and the opportunity to make a visible impact within a large and evolving digital environment. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Continuous G10 level role attracts a remuneration of $152,905 - $167,669 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume A document addressing the selection criteria for the role If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Wednesday, 11 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies, at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920732": {
      "title": "Safer Community Officers - Kalgoorlie",
      "description": "Safer Community Officers - Kalgoorlie Office of Corporate Services Kalgoorlie, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 28/10/2026 Competitive package + 17% super and regional allowances Help shape Curtin's new in-house Safer Community service Permanent full-time role with rent support and generous leave Job Reference: 1692770 An Exciting Opportunity for 2027! With our new in-house Safer Community service set to launch in early 2027, now is the perfect time to explore your next career opportunity with Curtin University. We're recruiting ahead of this exciting transition, giving successful candidates the opportunity to join a newly established team and help shape the future of our Safer Community service at the Kalgoorlie Campus. About the Role We are seeking Safer Community Officers to join Curtin University as we establish our in-house Safer Community service at the Kalgoorlie Campus. As a Safer Community Officer, you will provide a visible, approachable and reassuring presence across campus, supporting the safety, security and wellbeing of students, staff and visitors. While security is an important part of the role, Curtin’s Safer Community approach goes further. You will combine proactive patrols and incident response with strong customer service, early intervention and wellbeing support. No two shifts will be quite the same. You may be responding to a safety or security incident, supporting someone experiencing distress, providing a safety escort, assisting with events, liaising with emergency services or simply being a trusted and visible point of contact for members of the campus community. Key Responsibilities: Patrol campus on foot, bicycle and vehicle. Provide safety escorts and an approachable point of contact. Respond to incidents and assist emergency services. Triage wellbeing concerns and connect people with support. Record incidents and assist with events and traffic management. Skills & Experience: Certificate II in Security Operations from a recognised provider. Current Western Australian driver’s licence and Senior First Aid certificate. Strong communication and customer-service skills. Responds sensitively to people from diverse backgrounds. Available for an operational shift roster and fit for patrol duties. Mental Health First Aid training is desirable. Role Requirements Ability to work outside normal office hours and participate in an operational shift roster. Successful applicants will be subject to a National Police Clearance and background, integrity and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Properties Facilities & Development Curtin’s Properties Facilities & Development team supports the safe, effective and reliable operation of the University’s physical environment. In Kalgoorlie, the team provides facilities and maintenance support across the campus, student accommodation and Curtin’s expanding residential housing portfolio. Working across everything from laboratories and lecture theatres to accommodation and residential properties, you will be exposed to a variety of facilities and specialised environments, while working closely with university staff, contractors and service providers to keep our facilities safe, functional and well maintained. Curtin Kalgoorlie is located in the heart of Western Australia’s historic Goldfields. Home to the renowned WA School of Mines, the campus has a strong community atmosphere and provides the opportunity to live and work in one of Australia’s most important resources regions. What We Offer You Competitive remuneration: $69,000–$75,046 (G03) plus 17% superannuation, applicable locality and shift allowances. Locality allowance: $2,354 per annum, with increased entitlements for eligible employees with dependants. Additional earning opportunities: Potential overtime payments, subject to operational requirements. Generous leave entitlements: 20 days' annual leave, increasing to 25 days for eligible shift workers, plus 14 days' personal leave and 17.5% annual leave loading. Regional benefits: Two additional days of annual leave when travelling to Perth during annual leave, subject to eligibility. Family-friendly benefits: Up to 26 weeks paid parental leave for eligible primary caregivers. Additional leave: Five days' Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander leave for eligible employees, plus study leave and community service leave provisions. Extra time off: University-observed public holidays, including Easter Tuesday, and two additional University Days over Christmas/New Year, subject to roster arrangements. Employee discounts: Salary packaging through Paywise and access to selected hotel and health insurance discounts. Long-service leave: Potential recognition of eligible prior service. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit: https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join us! Please click APPLY NOW , ensuring you attach the following documents: Cover letter outlining your skills, knowledge and experience relevant to the position. Resume. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our Careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 9.00pm AWST, 12 November 2026 Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920312": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 27/10/2026 Help shape the future of assessment and student learning at Curtin University Full-time, fixed-term opportunity until 31 December 2027 | Bentley Campus Play a key role in the next phase of Curtin's university-wide Assessment 2030 in Job Reference: 1699927 Are you passionate about transforming the way students learn and are assessed? Do you enjoy collaborating with academics and educational leaders to challenge traditional approaches and drive meaningful improvements in higher education? Curtin University is seeking an experienced and innovative Senior Lecturer, Assessment Design to join our Assessment 2030 initiative, supporting the next phase of this exciting university-wide project. About the Role Working closely with the Faculty of Science and Engineering, you will play an important role in embedding innovative assessment practices, supporting curriculum redesign and helping academic teams deliver meaningful improvements to the student learning experience. This is a fantastic opportunity to build on the progress already achieved, influence assessment practices across the faculty and contribute to lasting improvements in teaching and learning at Curtin. Key Responsibilities Lead assessment and curriculum redesign initiatives across selected Science and Engineering courses, championing innovative and evidence-based approaches. Work collaboratively with academic leaders, Course Coordinators and teaching teams to review assessment practices and identify opportunities for improvement. Facilitate workshops and collaborative discussions to support course redesign, assessment innovation and curriculum alignment. Develop and finalise course-level assessment maps, providing valuable insights to inform academic decision-making. Support course teams in implementing redesigned assessments and translating recommendations into practical improvements. Evaluate the impact of assessment initiatives, contribute to project reporting and support the successful transition of project outcomes into ongoing faculty practices. SELECTION CRITERIA We are looking for an experienced and forward-thinking academic who is passionate about improving the student learning experience through innovative assessment and curriculum design. You will bring strong analytical and problem-solving skills, sound judgement and a collaborative approach to working with academic stakeholders. With excellent communication and interpersonal skills, you will be confident engaging with colleagues at all levels, building relationships and influencing positive change. To be successful in this role, you will demonstrate: A doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline, or equivalent accreditation and professional standing where applicable. Demonstrated expertise in innovative assessment and curriculum design within a higher education environment. Strong knowledge of contemporary educational theories and practices, including constructive alignment, universal design for learning and assessment validity. Proven experience designing innovative higher education courses and units, incorporating educational technologies and/or Generative AI tools. Strong analytical skills, including experience interpreting educational data, course enrolment and retention information, and course-level assessment mapping. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively with academic teams and stakeholders, including in complex or challenging environments. Role Requirements As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check Assessment 2030 Assessment 2030 is a strategic, university-wide initiative focused on transforming the design, delivery and evaluation of assessment at Curtin University. Guided by principles of assessment validity, inclusion and viability, the project aims to create meaningful assessment experiences that support student success, academic integrity and graduate employability. Led by Professor Mollie Dollinger and supported by the Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor, Academic, the project brings together academic leaders, educators and professional colleagues to drive positive change across the University. As the project enters its second phase in 2027, the focus will shift towards embedding redesigned assessments within faculties, supporting course teams through implementation and ensuring the improvements achieved can be sustained into the future. You will work centrally within the Assessment 2030 team while building strong collaborative relationships with colleagues across the Faculty of Science and Engineering, contributing to an initiative that will have a lasting impact on teaching and learning at Curtin. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. F or more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Fixed Term ALC level role attracts a remuneration of $144,512 - $166,354 (ALC) + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), 10 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920519": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer / Lecturer - Midwifery",
      "description": "Associate Lecturer / Lecturer - Midwifery Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 27/10/2026 Full time, or part time (0.6FTE minimum), continuing (permanent) Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions | Curtin Bentley Campus Top 100 in the world for Nursing (QS University Rankings by Subject 2026) Reference Number: 1697342 About the Role: The School of Nursing is seeking a knowledgeable and enthusiastic Midwifery Associate Lecturer / Lecturer to join our multi award-winning Midwifery team in a full or part time (0.6 FTE minimum), continuing (permanent) teaching-focused position . In this role, you will contribute to the delivery and coordination of specialised midwifery units across our Bachelor and Graduate Entry Midwifery programs, creating engaging learning experiences that connect contemporary scope-fulfilled midwifery practice with high-quality teaching. You will contribute to curriculum and learning resource development, assessment and feedback, while providing guidance and support to students throughout their studies. Drawing on your professional expertise and knowledge of contemporary midwifery practice, you will bring current industry perspectives into the learning environment and contribute to innovative approaches to teaching and curriculum development. You will also work collaboratively with colleagues across the School, contributing to a supportive academic environment and the ongoing enhancement of the student learning experience. This is an exciting opportunity for an experienced midwifery professional and educator to share their expertise and help prepare confident, capable graduates for contemporary midwifery practice. This position will commence in January 2027. Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Registration as a midwife with the Nursing and Midwifery Board of Australia Progress towards a doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline. Applicants seeking appointment at Lecturer (ALB) level will need to have completed a doctoral qualification (or equivalent accreditation and professional standing, where applicable) If you do not hold a PhD qualification, please outline your equivalent experience in your cover letter with reference to the following document: https://tinyurl.com/3r6yfaz4 (copy and paste link into web browser) Demonstrated ability to deliver high-quality teaching, with a reflective approach and commitment to continuous improvement in teaching practice Sound understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice relevant to the discipline, with a strong understanding expected at Lecturer (ALB) level Demonstrated commitment to scholarship in teaching and professional development in higher education, with an emerging record of contribution to teaching scholarship expected at Lecturer (ALB) level Demonstrated ability to contribute to curriculum review, innovation and development, with experience or capacity to undertake unit coordination expected at Lecturer (ALB) level High-level communication, interpersonal and organisational skills, with the ability to contribute to a collegial and supportive working environment and engage effectively with students and staff from diverse backgrounds Please note that if your qualifications or experience look different from what we've described above, but you feel your skills and potential are a match to the capabilities we need, please apply. We are looking for diversity in experience. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction School of Nursing The Curtin School of Nursing is the longest-established nursing school in the Australian university sector. Since opening in 1974, the school has played a central role in Curtin’s leadership in health sciences education and research. Today, we rank in the top 100 in the world for Nursing in the QS World University Rankings by Subject 2026. The school delivers undergraduate courses in midwifery, nursing, oral health therapy and paramedicine; and specialist postgraduate courses in areas ranging from critical care to child health and midwifery. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This full or part time (0.6 FTE minimum), permanent (teaching only) role attracts a remuneration of $85,872 - $115,862 (ALA) / $121,857 - $144,352 (ALB), plus 17% superannuation *Please note that each candidate will be assessed based on their skills, qualifications, and experience to determine the salary level. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than four pages o Please confirm your preferred FTE – 1 FTE to 0.6 FTE minimum o Please also advise on the level you are applying for – ALA / ALB If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920221": {
      "title": "Director of Primary Care (General Practice, Rural and Remote)",
      "description": "Director of Primary Care (General Practice, Rural and Remote) Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 27/10/2026 Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Remuneration package including clinical loading, 17% super and generous leave Full-time, Continuing (part-time, job share arrangements considered) Job reference: 1674007 Lead primary care, rural and remote medical education within Curtin Medical School. Provide academic leadership to Curtin’s Rural Health Campus in Kalgoorlie and oversee the Commonwealth-funded Rural Health Multidisciplinary Training program. Build meaningful partnerships that strengthen rural, remote and Aboriginal health education and outcomes. Help shape the future of primary care and rural medical education Curtin Medical School is seeking an experienced and highly collaborative academic leader to join us as Director of Primary Care (General Practice, Rural and Remote) . This is an influential role for someone who is passionate about preparing future doctors to meet the needs of diverse communities across Western Australia. You will provide strategic and academic leadership across primary care, rural and remote medical education, while building strong relationships with healthcare providers, communities, government and educational partners. A focus of this position is supporting Curtin’s Rural Health Multidisciplinary Training (RHMT) program . You will oversee the program’s educational delivery, funding and commitments, while working closely with colleagues at our Bentley campus and providing academic leadership and support to Curtin’s Rural Health Campus in Kalgoorlie. As a member of the Medical School’s Senior Leadership Group, you will also help shape broader strategic priorities and contribute to a collegial, inclusive and future-focused School culture. About the role As Director of Primary Care, you will: Provide strategic and academic leadership across general practice, primary care, rural and remote medical education. Support the RHMT program, ensuring high-quality delivery and compliance with funding, application and reporting requirements. Provide academic leadership and support to the Rural Health Campus in Kalgoorlie. Strengthen Curtin’s rural health education and its contribution to regional workforce capability. Grow and sustain trusted partnerships with rural and remote communities, healthcare providers, government agencies and other external stakeholders. Advance primary care education and initiatives within the Discipline of Medicine. Champion Aboriginal health education and advocacy, working respectfully and collaboratively to strengthen outcomes for Aboriginal communities. Lead curriculum initiatives and pedagogical developments that enhance student learning, graduate capability and readiness for contemporary medical practice. Promote teaching practices that embed cultural diversity, equity and inclusion throughout the curriculum. Contribute to educational policy and professional discussions at state and national levels. Mentor and support academic colleagues, including through peer review and the sharing of effective teaching practice. Contribute to the leadership, service, engagement and collegial life of Curtin Medical School and the wider University. About you You will be an established academic or clinical education leader who brings credibility, sound judgement and a genuine commitment to primary care and rural and remote health. Your leadership style will be relational and inclusive. You will be comfortable bringing people together around a shared purpose, navigating complex stakeholder environments and supporting colleagues to do their best work. Just as importantly, you will understand the value of listening to communities and building partnerships grounded in trust, respect and reciprocity. Selection Criteria You will bring: A qualification in medicine. A PhD and current registration with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (AHPRA) are highly desirable. Demonstrated excellence in teaching, supported by a reflective and evidence-informed approach. A significant contribution to the scholarship of teaching and learning, including experience in curriculum development and unit/ course coordination. The ability to lead major curriculum and translate strategy into meaningful outcomes. Demonstrated academic leadership within your discipline, together with a strong record of community and stakeholder engagement. Highly developed communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to foster a collegial and supportive working environment. The ability to engage respectfully and effectively with students, colleagues and communities from diverse cultural backgrounds. Role Requirements As this is a continuing contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. While this is a full time position , p art-time, job-share arrangements may be considered for the right candidate/s. Applicants should be clear in their application as to their availability / preferred FTE. About the Team: Curtin Medical School Curtin Medical School delivers Western Australia’s only undergraduate MBBS, enrolling up to 120 students annually with a strong emphasis on equity, rural health, and Indigenous participation. Our future-focused curriculum is delivered through state-of-the-art clinical teaching and simulation facilities at Bentley, supported by an extensive clinical education network that includes: Over 10 metropolitan hospital placement sites Health campuses in Midland and Kalgoorlie Membership in the Rural Clinical School of WA Strong partnerships with metropolitan and rural primary care and community providers We are committed to producing future-ready doctors with strong primary care capability, leadership skills, and a commitment to serving diverse communities. For more information, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/learning-teaching/health-sciences/curtin-medical-school/ About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You This full-time continuing position attracts a remuneration package of $148,847 - $171,344 (Senior Lecturer - ALC) / $178,845 - $196,832 (Associate Professor - ALD) + *clinical loading + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and a variety of food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and how you meet the selection criteria for the role (noted above) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday 22 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920029": {
      "title": "Lecturer- Senior Lecturer (S50D) | School of Design & Built Environment",
      "description": "Lecturer- Senior Lecturer (S50D) | School of Design & Built Environment Faculty of Humanities Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 26/10/2026 Continuing Lecturer and Senior Lecturer positions available Based at our vibrant Bentley campus Be part of enhancing cultural capability & make a difference! Job Reference: 1698898 At Curtin we are committed to embedding the voices and perspectives of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples at the forefront of our decision-making to create an environment in which everyone thrives. In line with Curtin’s 2030 Strategic Plan, Curtin University is committed to optimising employment outcomes for Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples. Curtin University considers that being an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person is a genuine occupational requirement for this position under section 50(d) of the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA). Therefore, only applications from Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people will be considered. Do you have a driving passion for cultural knowledge, community connection and supporting Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students to thrive? Curtin University’s School of Design and Built Environment is seeking Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander academics for continuing Lecturer and Senior Lecturer roles that will contribute to teaching, curriculum development, research and community engagement across design and built environment disciplines. These roles will contribute to culturally responsive learning, teaching and scholarship across DBE. These continuing Lecturer/Senior Lecturer roles are available on a full-time or part-time basis and attract remuneration at the ALB/ALC level as follows: Lecturer (ALB): $121,857 - $144,352 + 17% superannuation, pro rata for part-time appointments. Senior Lecturer (ALC): $148,847 - $171,344 + 17% superannuation, pro rata for part-time appointments. About the Role: As part of DBE, you will contribute to academic teaching, curriculum design, student learning, research or scholarship, and engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, industry and colleagues. The role will include supporting the development and delivery of culturally responsive learning experiences, including DBE core teaching and the postgraduate unit Boola Kaardidiny Bidi (INDS5016) , while also contributing to the broader academic life of the School. Teach and support student learning: Design, deliver and support culturally responsive teaching across DBE units, including learning experiences that reflect Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander knowledges, perspectives and ways of being. Develop curriculum and academic practice: Work with colleagues to develop curriculum, assessment and learning activities that strengthen Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander content and culturally safe approaches across design and built environment education. Build partnerships and contribute to scholarship: Engage with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, industry and academic colleagues to support culturally grounded teaching, research, scholarship and professional practice. Who We’re Looking For We are seeking academics who bring relevant disciplinary expertise, cultural capability, and experience in teaching, scholarship, research or professional practice aligned with DBE’s priorities. A key focus of the role will include contributing to learning and curriculum connected to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander knowledges within DBE, including the postgraduate unit Boola Kaardidiny Bidi (INDS5016) . This unit explores Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander histories, cultures, knowledge systems and contemporary experiences, with particular emphasis on Noongar perspectives, connections to Country, truth-telling, cultural protocols and social justice. Have Recognised Expertise: Bring relevant local, national or international expertise in practice, scholarship, research, teaching or leadership that supports the School’s priorities. Possess Diverse Skills: Show expertise in one or more of the following areas: Co-design and participatory design with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities Creative Practices : Fashion Design, Graphic Design, Industrial, Photography, Architecture, Interior Architecture, Landscape Architecture, & Urban Design Built Environment : Environmental Engineering / Science and Sustainability Leadership, Construction Management, Planning, Geography If your qualifications or experience look different from what we've described above, but you feel your skills and potential are a match to the capabilities we need, please apply. We are looking for diversity in experience, thought and approach. Make a Lasting Impact In these roles, you will help strengthen culturally safe learning environments and contribute to meaningful outcomes for students, communities and the broader design and built environment professions. About Curtin University’s School of Design and Built Environment DBE is a leader in innovative teaching and research, offering an extraordinary blend of disciplines that lead to impactful, real-world outcomes. At DBE, you will be part of a supportive team dedicated to advancing knowledge in design and built environments while strengthening community partnerships and making a positive difference. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2024 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ We offer more than just a job, with benefits that support wellbeing, flexibility and professional development. Adaptable work environment: Be based at our Bentley campus, with flexibility to work remotely or from other campus locations where suitable. We support flexible work arrangements, including the option for part-time. Rewards Beyond Salary: Enjoy the perks of a generous superannuation contribution (17%) with cash out options*, cultural language allowance, customisable salary packaging benefits through Paywise, as well as Hotel and Health Insurance discounts. Time Well-Spent: Experience the added value of 17.5% Leave Loading, with the option to be paid out or taken as additional leave. Benefit from Aboriginal Torres Strait Islander Leave (5 days), Annual Leave (20 days), Personal Leave (14 days), Parental Leave (26 weeks for the primary caregiver), Study Leave and Community Service Leave. Recognition for Prior Service: Curtin recognises prior service for long service leave purposes. New Curtin employees may* be eligible if they have worked for an Australian University, public service or other recognised institution. For more information about our university, please visit www.curtin.edu.au How to apply If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW and attach the following documents: Updated resume. Cover letter outlining your interest and experience. A document addressing the selection criteria (noted on page 5 of the candidate information brochure). Role requirements: As this is a continuing position, applicants must have permanent work rights in Australia to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. Please visit the following link to view the candidate information brochure: https://tinyurl.com/ya3endp2 If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm AWST, Tuesday 17th November 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=917452": {
      "title": "Animal Welfare Officer",
      "description": "Animal Welfare Officer Research Office at Curtin Perth, Western Australia Permanent Part Time 26/10/2026 Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions On-campus amenities including a gym, medical centre, and childcare facilities Part-time ongoing role 3 days per week (flexible on days) Reference: 1695530 About the Role: The Animal Welfare Officer plays a vital role in supporting ethical, high-quality teaching and research at Curtin by championing best-practice animal care and welfare. Authorised by the University, this position ensures activities are conducted in line with the NHMRC Australian Code for the Care and Use of Animals for Scientific Purposes and the decisions of the Curtin Animal Ethics Committee. You will provide senior-level advice and practical support on animal ethics, assist with protocol development, monitor animal welfare across the University, and help build awareness of responsible animal care in a collaborative research environment. Your responsibilities will include: Support Curtin’s teaching and research activities by ensuring animal care and use meets animal welfare legislation, the Australian Code, and Animal Ethics Committee requirements. Provide clear, practical advice to researchers, teaching staff, animal users, the Animal Ethics Committee, and the Research Integrity Office on animal welfare and ethics matters. Assist with animal ethics applications, protocol development, Standard Operating Procedures, and pre-application guidance for staff using animals in research or teaching. Coordinate and conduct routine inspections of Curtin animal holding facilities to help maintain high standards of animal welfare and best practice. Build strong working relationships with internal stakeholders and relevant local, national, and international agencies to stay informed on animal welfare codes, legislation, and expectations. Contribute to continuous improvement by supporting strategic advice, maintaining current knowledge of animal welfare requirements, and promoting responsible animal care across the University. Who we are looking for: We are looking for a knowledgeable and practical animal welfare professional who can confidently support ethical research and teaching, provide trusted advice, and help strengthen best-practice animal care across Curtin. Strong knowledge of animal welfare legislation, regulatory requirements, ethics governance, and best-practice standards. Confidence providing clear, practical advice to researchers, teaching staff, committees, and stakeholders with professionalism and cultural sensitivity. Excellent communication, planning, analytical, negotiation, and problem-solving skills. Experience developing practical training, Standard Operating Procedures, and guidance materials that help others understand and apply animal welfare requirements. A veterinary or other relevant qualification, supported by extensive experience in animal welfare, animal ethics, research, teaching, or a related field. A proactive, organised approach with the ability to work independently while contributing positively within a collaborative team environment. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Continuous Part-time G08 level role attracts a remuneration of $122,699 - $130,758 pro rata to 0.6 FTE + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday, 8 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920008": {
      "title": "Station Coordinator - Curtin FM",
      "description": "Station Coordinator - Curtin FM Office of Corporate Services Perth, Western Australia Permanent Part Time 26/10/2026 Continuing, part-time opportunity at 0.6 FTE with Curtin FM On-campus amenities including a gym, medical centre, and childcare facilities Generous leave entitlements plus 17% superannuation Reference: 1669670 About the Role: Bring together the people, systems and activity that keep Curtin FM 100.1, Curtin University’s community radio station, running smoothly each day. Working within the strategy and direction set by the Associate Director, Curtin FM, you will coordinate staff, volunteers and subcontractors while supporting a vibrant community broadcasting service. This varied role connects station operations with on-air production, compliance, sales and sponsorship activity, listener engagement and fundraising. You will help translate priorities into well-organised delivery, including outside broadcasts and the station’s membership and volunteer programs. Your responsibilities will include: Coordinate the day-to-day activities of Curtin FM staff, volunteers and subcontractors under the direction of the Associate Director, Curtin FM. Support sales and sponsorship activity against approved targets, including subcontractor arrangements, monthly targets, commission preparation, lead allocation and performance reporting. Assist with daily on-air compliance monitoring and production support, helping presenters understand and meet their obligations under the Community Broadcasting Codes of Practice. Coordinate volunteer processes, including recruitment, retention and the biannual survey. Help organise outside broadcasts and coordinate the annual Radiothon, membership renewals and bequest activity with the University’s Advancement function. Assist in the coordination of the annual online listener survey and maintain the station’s website and social media presence. Respond constructively to listener queries and complaints, escalating complex matters to the Associate Director, Curtin FM. Who we are looking (Selection Criteria): You are an organised, collaborative coordinator who can keep multiple workstreams moving while building trusted relationships with staff, volunteers, subcontractors, sponsors, listeners and community partners. Essential: A relevant qualification in media, communications, business or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education or training. Demonstrated experience coordinating day-to-day operations across multiple functions in a fast-paced environment. Experience managing or coordinating staff, subcontractors or volunteers, including scheduling, timesheet approval and basic performance conversations. Experience implementing sales, sponsorship or fundraising activity against targets set by others. Strong interpersonal skills, including the ability to build rapport with a large and diverse volunteer cohort and respond constructively to listener feedback and complaints. A working understanding of on-air compliance requirements for community broadcasting, or the ability to acquire this knowledge quickly. Desirable: Experience in a community broadcasting station, radio station or similar volunteer-based media organisation. Experience coordinating fundraising events or campaigns, such as a radiothon or membership drive. Experience in a university or other large, complex organisation. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Ability to work outside of normal office hours when required Anticipated start date: January 2027 Your new Team: Curtin FM Curtin FM is Curtin University’s community radio station. In this role, you will work closely with the Associate Director, Curtin FM, and coordinate across station staff, volunteers, subcontractors, sales representatives, University Advancement, listeners, the broader community and technical support. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Continuous G07 level role attracts a remuneration of $109,277-$117,331 per annum, pro rata for 0.6 FTE + 17% superannuation Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday, 8th of November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=919477": {
      "title": "Project Coordinator",
      "description": "Project Coordinator Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Contract Part Time 24/10/2026 Part time (0.8FTE), fixed term until 28 September 2029| based at Technology Park Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Reference Number: 1691676 About the Role: Be part of a nationally significant research project seeking to better understand polysubstance use and new psychoactive substances among young Australians and inform practical, evidence-based harm reduction responses. Based within Curtin’s National Drug Research Institute (NDRI), the Project Coordinator will work alongside researchers, health services and people with lived experience to help deliver an innovative three-year MRFF-funded study across WA, Victoria and the ACT. Working closely with Perth based chief investigators, you will coordinate project activities, monitor timelines and quality, and contribute to qualitative and quantitative research. This role would suit a motivated, emerging researcher with experience in project coordination, public health or alcohol and other drug (AOD) research, who is enthusiastic about harm reduction and collaborative, co-designed research with real-world impact. This is a part time (up to 0.8 FTE, offering flexibility to negotiate your own working schedule), fixed-term position until 28 September 2029. Based in Technology Park, which is adjacent to Curtin University’s Bentley Campus and is expected to commence in January 2027. Your responsibilities will include: Coordinating the day-to-day operations of the project across three data collection jurisdictions and the UTAS quantitative data analysis hub Managing project meetings and logistics, liaising with partner organisations, and tracking and supporting project progress Contributing to the collection, coordination and collation of research data Supporting ethics submissions and responses, alongside other project administration and reporting requirements Monitoring project activities, timelines and deliverables to support effective implementation across the three-year project Contributing to all phases of the research project, working collaboratively with the broader project team and partners Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Demonstrated project coordination or project management experience, with the ability to manage project plans, timelines and competing priorities Experience with qualitative and/or quantitative research methods, including contributing to research data collection and management Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build effective relationships and liaise sensitively with diverse stakeholders and partner organisations Experience working with people with lived experience of drug use and using co-design approaches, with appropriate sensitivity and respect Experience in the alcohol and other drug (AOD) sector or public health research Strong written communication, organisational and problem-solving skills, with excellent attention to detail and the ability to manage competing priorities and deadlines Desirable: Knowledge of drug use among young people in festival settings Project coordination experience in a research setting Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Ability to work outside of normal office hours when required Your new Team: National Drug Research Institute (NDRI) The National Drug Research Institute (NDRI) is one of the largest centres of alcohol and other drug research expertise in Australia. The Institute’s purpose is to conduct and disseminate research that supports evidence-informed policy and practice to prevent and minimise alcohol and other drug-related health, social, cultural and economic harms. NDRI research has informed and contributed significantly to policy and evidence-based practice in Australia and internationally. Learn more about our team at https://ndri.curtin.edu.au/ About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This part time (up to 0.8 FTE), fixed term (until 28 September 2029) G07 level role attracts a remuneration of $109,277 - $117,331 pro rata to 0.8 FTE + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, this role is based at Curtin’s Technology Park, adjacent to our vibrant Bentley campus. With the campus close by, you’ll have easy access to an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and a wide range of food options, including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life nearby, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=919502": {
      "title": "Faculty Business Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Faculty Business Operations Coordinator Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 24/10/2026 Full time, fixed term contract until October 2028 | Curtin Bentley Campus Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Reference Number: 1679361 About the Role: We are seeking an experienced and proactive individual to join the Faculty of Health Sciences as Faculty Business Operations Coordinator. You will coordinate operational and administrative functions for the Faculty Office, support a small team and work closely with internal service providers to deliver efficient and responsive services. This is an opportunity to make your mark, with scope to review existing ways of working, simplify processes and develop efficient, well-documented systems that support consistent service delivery. You will have the opportunity to identify improvements, take initiative and help shape how the Faculty operates. The successful candidate will bring strong planning, problem-solving and organisational skills, a process-driven approach and the ability to build effective relationships and motivate a small team to achieve positive outcomes. If you enjoy a dynamic environment and are motivated by improving processes and making a tangible difference, we encourage you to apply. Your responsibilities will include: Manage day-to-day business operations across assigned Faculty areas. Build effective relationships with University service functions and Faculty stakeholders. Develop and improve business processes to support efficient service delivery. Coordinate projects, initiatives and Faculty-wide communications. Provide executive support, including reports, briefing papers and committee support. Manage operational budgets, reporting and resource allocation. Lead and manage the administrative pool, including staffing and performance. Who we are looking for (selection criteria): Advanced interpersonal and oral communication skills with the ability to effectively consult with a wide range of stakeholders on a range of matters and in a professional and confident manner. Strong organisational skills with the ability to manage multiple deliverables in a dynamic and complex environment. Developed analytical and problem solving skills with the ability to offer solutions to complex problems. Experience managing staff, with the ability to conduct effective work planning and performance review discussions and to foster a work environment of high achievement, continuous improvement, and job satisfaction. Demonstrated understanding of financial and budget management. High level written communication skills with the ability to prepare high quality reports, briefing papers and correspondence. Desirable : Previous experience working within the higher education sector. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This is a full time, fixed term contract until October 2028, with a salary range between $109,277 - $117,331 (G07) per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Sunday, 08 November 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=919519": {
      "title": "Accommodation Program Manager",
      "description": "Accommodation Program Manager Office of Corporate Services Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 24/10/2026 Full-time, continuing (permanent) position | Bentley Campus Competitive salary and generous leave entitlements + 17% superannuation On-campus amenities including a gym, medical centre, and childcare facilities Reference: 1698931 About the Role: Support the effective delivery of Curtin’s accommodation portfolio in a role that brings together program leadership, project coordination, service improvement and stakeholder engagement. Reporting to the Senior Manager Accommodation, you will lead the Accommodation Coaches and coordinate programs that connect accommodation priorities with the University’s wider strategy. You will help strengthen student experience, wellbeing, safety and community connection across on-campus and off-campus accommodation settings, including supporting the implementation of Curtin’s commitments under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence. Working closely with Curtin teams, external partners and accommodation providers, you will translate strategic priorities into practical plans, coordinated delivery and meaningful reporting. Your responsibilities will include: Coordinate the delivery of accommodation programs and initiatives that support the student journey and align with portfolio objectives and Curtin’s strategy. Provide day to day leadership to the Accommodation Coaches, building team capability and supporting consistent, responsive and student-centred services. Plan and deliver assigned programs and projects, agreeing timelines and monitoring progress, milestones and outcomes. Coordinate relevant compliance, health and safety, occupancy, risk and quality assurance reporting and audits to inform program delivery and service improvement. Develop and improve business processes, procedures and operational plans with the Senior Manager Accommodation, translating strategic priorities into practical service improvements. Support budget monitoring, requisitions and payments within approved delegations, and prepare business cases and project briefs. Build collaborative partnerships across the University and with accommodation providers, and support the monitoring, recording and resolution of accommodation-related complaints and incidents with sound judgement and discretion. Who we are looking for (Selection Criteria): You will bring the judgement, organisation and collaborative leadership needed to manage competing priorities in a complex service environment. To be successful, you will have: Postgraduate qualifications, or progress towards them, with extensive relevant experience; extensive experience and management expertise in an accommodation field; or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience. Demonstrated ability to develop, implement and manage program plans within budget and resource constraints. Strong organisational skills and the ability to manage multiple deliverables and deadlines in a dynamic environment. Sound financial and budget management capability. Demonstrated experience leading and supporting staff and improving business processes to deliver effective, student-centred services. High-level interpersonal, consultation, written and oral communication skills, including the ability to prepare quality reports, briefings and correspondence. The ability to work collaboratively, analyse complex matters, manage sensitive issues with discretion and connect program delivery with broader organisational priorities. Desirable: Experience in student accommodation, student support or community development within the tertiary education sector, and an understanding of student wellbeing and the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction Ability to work outside normal office hours when required Travel to regional Western Australia, interstate and/or overseas may be required Your new Team: Accommodation & Community Services Accommodation is a portfolio within Accommodation & Community Services. The Accommodation team supports students through accommodation advice and coaching, residential community initiatives and programs that promote wellbeing, safety and connection. In this role, you will work closely with the Agricola student accommodation team in Kalgoorlie, across Student Life & Experience, Properties, Facilities and Development, Finance and other University teams, as well as accommodation providers and external partners. You will connect accommodation programs with wider University priorities and support coordinated delivery across the portfolio. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Continuous (permanent) G08 level role attracts a remuneration of $122,699 - $130,758 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday, 8th of November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
