    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=919515": {
      "title": "Events Coordinator",
      "description": "Events Coordinator Office of the Provost Perth, Western Australia Contract Part Time 24/10/2026 Part-time, 0.8 FTE, fixed term to June 2028 l Bentley Campus Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Anticipated start date: January 2027 Reference: 1688004 About the Role: The Australian Centre for Student Equity and Success (ACSES ) is seeking an experienced and highly organised Events Coordinator to plan and deliver events that support our priorities, stakeholder engagement, and sector impact. Working under the broad direction of the Communications and Stakeholder Engagement Manager, you will coordinate events from initial planning through to post-event evaluation. This includes managing logistics, registrations, speakers, venues, suppliers, budgets, and on-the-day delivery. You will also prepare event-related communications and contribute to broader communications activities as part of the ACSES Communications team. Your responsibilities will include: Plan and coordinate in-person, online, and hybrid events from concept to completion. Manage event logistics, including venues, catering, audio-visual requirements, accessibility, suppliers, and event materials. Prepare event documentation, including schedules, run sheets, briefings, attendee lists, and risk documentation. Manage registrations and communications with attendees, speakers, facilitators, and other contributors. Monitor budgets, obtain quotes, process invoices, and maintain accurate records. Oversee on-the-day delivery, including registrations, speaker support, supplier coordination, program timing, and troubleshooting. Liaise professionally with internal and external stakeholders, including sector partners, government representatives, and suppliers. Prepare event-related communications and promotional content across digital and print channels. Evaluate event delivery and recommend improvements for future activities. Who we are looking for: You will be an adaptable and detail-oriented events professional with experience coordinating events through their full lifecycle. You will be comfortable managing competing priorities, working with diverse stakeholders, and responding calmly when circumstances change. You will also have strong written communication skills and an understanding of the importance of accessible, inclusive, and professionally delivered events. Selection Criteria To be considered for this position, your application should clearly address the following selection criteria. Please use specific examples to demonstrate your experience. Essential Demonstrated experience coordinating and delivering in-person, online, and hybrid events from planning through to completion. Demonstrated ability to anticipate and resolve problems during event planning and delivery, exercise sound judgement, and respond calmly when circumstances change. Experience preparing clear event-related communications for channels such as registration platforms, newsletters, email, and social media. Strong organisational and project coordination skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, maintain accurate event documentation, and meet firm deadlines. High-level interpersonal and communication skills, with demonstrated experience coordinating speakers and liaising professionally with senior stakeholders, event participants, colleagues, and external suppliers. Desirable : Knowledge or experience of the Australian tertiary education sector. Broader experience contributing to communications and stakeholder engagement activities. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. Employment type: 0.8 FTE, with consideration given to full-time (1.0 FTE) for the right candidate. Anticipated start date: January 2027. Your new Team: Australian Centre for Student Equity and Success (ACSES ) is a national research and policy centre funded by the Australian Government Department of Education and based at Curtin University. Our core mission is to bridge the gap between research, policy, and practice in higher education to promote equity. We conduct a wide range of activities, including research projects, grants programs, policy evaluation, fellowships, data analysis, publications, events, and more. We are committed to building a robust evidence base that informs evidence-based policy and institutional best practices, making a real impact on higher education accessibility. For more information on ACSES visit www.acses.edu.au, or for more information about Curtin, please visit www.curtin.edu.au. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Fixed Term G06 level role attracts a remuneration of $99,213 - $105,251 pro rata for (0.8) FTE + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Sunday, 8th of November 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=918993": {
      "title": "Project Administrator",
      "description": "Project Administrator Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Contract Part Time 22/10/2026 Fixed term, part-time (0.6 FTE) contract until 30 June 2028 Work alongside engineers, astronomers, data scientists and technical experts On-campus amenities including a gym, medical centre, and childcare facilities Reference Number: 1697334 About the Team: Radio Astronomy applies ‘STEM’ based disciplines to capture and analyse ultra-faint signals from the universe. In doing this, we push the boundaries of technology and techniques in electromagnetics, signal processing, software development, and radio frequency communications. The Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy (CIRA) and The International Centre for Radio Astronomy Research (ICRAR) deliver a dedicated Translation and Impact program (T&I) to support the translation of our core astronomy and engineering expertise, skills, knowledge, and technology for impact in industries like space and defence - to name a few. The T&I team enables ICRAR’s technical staff to deliver impact by providing broad support to the technical experts who deliver on collaborative projects. About the Role: As Project Administrator, you will provide high level administrative and operational project support to the T&I program and individual industry-research projects, assisting with the development, coordination, and implementation of project deliverables under the guidance of the project lead. To excel in providing operational project support, you will draw on previous exposure to a technical or scientific discipline to understand CIRA’s engineering capabilities. As part of the multi-disciplinary T&I team, you will engage with diverse stakeholders – from academic researchers and students (engineers, astronomers, data scientists, software developers) – to industry and government professionals (including defence, space, computing, resources, climate, weather, environment).To succeed in this, you will need to be responsive and service oriented, with strong written and verbal communication skills and excellent attention to detail. Administratively, you will support the T&I program and industry research projects with excellent organisational skills, attention to detail in document management, planning and tracking activities for reporting purposes, and ensuring quality and accuracy in all communications. You will be responsible for creating materials which are visually compelling, and tailored to relevant audiences. Your responsibilities will include: Coordinating and preparing regular and ad hoc project reports, e.g. status reports and end of month reporting requirements, such as project forecasting expenditure information and accruals. Maintaining project registers, databases, artefacts and documentation. Liaising with relevant external stakeholders and University service functions (e.g. People and Culture, Financial Services) to ensure relevant actions are completed. Working under the guidance of the project lead to support technical project delivery teams with: field trip preparation and logistics, including assistance in preparing risk assessments and safety protocols; procurement of technical services and supplies; and preparation of documents required for the export of certain technical goods and services, including Defence Export Controls and other customs requirements, ensuring compliance with Curtin University policies and procedures. Liaising with project teams to facilitate data capture and verifying milestones/activity. Facilitating information flow, administering communications and correspondence and distributing visually compelling and precise information in a professional and timely manner. Who We Are Looking For (Selection Criteria): We are looking for someone personable and driven who may see this as an opportunity to advance their project or program management aspirations. In this role, your previous exposure to a physical science, computing, or engineering discipline will complement your business administration and operations support. You will be comfortable working alongside technical subject matter experts to understand and communicate effectively our industry-relevant capabilities. Additionally, you will demonstrate skills and experience in the following Essential Criteria : Demonstrated ability to gather, synthesise, and present written and visually compelling material for a variety of audiences. Completion of a relevant degree; or an equivalent combination of relevant work experience and/or education/training. Demonstrated excellent organisational skills with the ability to organise time and work activities according to priorities and to meet deadlines. Demonstrated interpersonal and oral communication skills with the ability to consult and liaise effectively with a diverse range of people and with cross cultural sensitivity. Demonstrated effective written communication skills with the ability to construct clear and concise correspondence and documentation. Proven ability to work well in a team, including respecting difference, leading, supporting others, accepting direction, and working to group goals. Demonstrated research and problem-solving skills. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role to be considered. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction This is a part-time role – up to 0.6FTE; we will consider requests for flexible working hours including requests to work over 3 to 5 days a week. Your new Team – CIRA: The Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy (CIRA) is a multi-disciplinary research institute engaged in a range of projects involving or motivated by radio astronomy. CIRA’s activities draw together academic research and professional staff from a variety of science, engineering, and computing disciplines to tackle unique and demanding challenges in astronomy and non-astronomy domains. Committed to research translation and impact (T&I), CIRA is committed to engaging with partners to share our skills, knowledge, and expertise for mutual benefit and impact beyond astronomy. CIRA is the lead organisation and operator of the Murchison Widefield Array (MWA), a key participant in the international SKA Project. CIRA is also the Curtin University node of the International Centre for Radio Astronomy Research (ICRAR) which was established in 2009, and one of the largest and most enduring academic research initiatives in Western Australia. CIRA celebrates diversity and is an equal opportunity employer. We encourage applications from all cultures, races, nationalities, genders, identities, and abilities aligning with the selection criteria, and are committed to preserving our principles of equitable and inclusive work and study environments. To learn more about CIRA, please visit the below websites: https://research.curtin.edu.au/cira/ www.icrar.org About Curtin University We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This fixed term, part-time G05 level role attracts a remuneration of $85,781 - $93,836 (pro rata at 0.6 FTE) + 17% superannuation . Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest, experience and addressing the section criteria for the role (no more than two pages) Applications close: 8pm, Monday, 9 November 2026 If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=918081": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Teaching and Research",
      "description": "Lecturer - Teaching and Research Faculty of Humanities Perth, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 21/10/2026 Ongoing role, Full-time, Bentley Campus Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Diverse & inclusive environment that fosters innovation & collaboration Reference: 1696073 About the Role: The School of Media, Creative Arts and Social Inquiry (MCASI) at Curtin University is seeking a Lecturer (Academic Level B) in Libraries, Archives, Records and Information Science (LARIS). Based at our Bentley campus in Perth, this Teaching and Research position spans two closely connected discipline areas: Library and Information Science and Recordkeeping and Archives. You will contribute to postgraduate teaching, unit coordination and research, helping prepare students for professional practice in libraries, archives, records management and information services. Working with colleagues and industry partners, you will connect teaching and research with contemporary professional practice and support opportunities for students to gain workplace experience. In this role, you will: Deliver high-quality postgraduate teaching and undertake unit coordination across Library and Information Science, and Recordkeeping and Archives, including assessment, curriculum development and ongoing reflective improvement of teaching practice. Develop a coherent program of high-quality research within LARIS, publish in refereed journals and other discipline-appropriate outlets, contribute to competitive research funding applications, disseminate findings through conferences and other scholarly forums, and contribute to the development of teaching and research programs. Build collaborative relationships with industry, national and state organisations, and research end-users. Support work-integrated learning opportunities that connect students with professional practice. Contribute to academic service, professional and community engagement, and a collegial and supportive working environment, consistent with University policies and professional standards. Contribute to the supervision of PhD and master’s research students. Selection Criteria: Your application should demonstrate how you meet the following essential selection criteria: A doctoral qualification and relevant expertise in Library and Information Science, Archives and/or Recordkeeping. Demonstrated high-level teaching skills and a reflective approach to delivering and continuously improving teaching. A strong understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice relevant to the discipline, together with a record of contribution to the scholarship of teaching. An established record of research outputs in high-quality refereed journals or other discipline-appropriate outlets. A history of successful competitive grant applications. Demonstrated ability to supervise higher degree research students. Demonstrated experience in, or capacity to undertake, unit coordination. Demonstrated high-level communication and interpersonal skills, a collaborative approach to academic work, a commitment to fostering a collegial and supportive working environment, and the ability to interact with students and staff with cross-cultural sensitivity. Demonstrated commitment to applying relevant policies, procedures and legislation in the day-to-day responsibilities of the position. Desirable: Professional experience as a librarian, archivist, information scientist or records manager is desirable. About us The Libraries, Archives, Records and Information Science (LARIS) area sits within the iSchool discipline of MCASI. Research across the iSchool encompasses open access and open science, social media and communications, research evaluation and more. Our staff have strong ties with other areas including Internet Studies, Journalism and the Curtin Open Knowledge Initiative (COKI), creating opportunities for collaboration across disciplines. MCASI is a member of iSchools Inc. and maintains links with international and national professional associations in the LARIS fields. Our courses connect critical information practice with global information issues and the Australian information sector. Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Continuous ALB level role attracts a remuneration of $121,857 - $144,352 + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) 2 - 4 Pages max If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Tuesday 10th of November 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=916379": {
      "title": "Safer Community Shift Supervisor - Kalgoorlie",
      "description": "Safer Community Shift Supervisor - Kalgoorlie Office of Corporate Services Kalgoorlie, Western Australia Permanent Full Time 15/10/2026 Full-time, continuing (permanent) opportunity | Kalgoorlie Campus Lead Curtin's newly established in-house Safer Community service Attractive regional benefits including locality allowance Job Reference: 1692764 About the Role We are seeking an experienced and motivated Safer Community Shift Supervisor to lead frontline operations and support the safety, security and wellbeing of the Curtin Kalgoorlie community. As Shift Supervisor, you will provide leadership and direction to a team of Safer Community Officers while overseeing day-to-day operations during rostered shifts. You will coordinate responses to safety, security and wellbeing incidents, support emergency management activities and ensure a high standard of service delivery across campus. This position combines traditional security leadership with a strong people-focused and wellbeing-centred approach. You will be joining the service as Curtin transitions Kalgoorlie security operations from an outsourced model to direct University delivery, providing an opportunity to help establish the team and contribute to how the service operates from the outset. The approved business case identifies improved student experience, greater operational oversight, enhanced governance and accountability, consistent Safer Communities service delivery and reduced operational and reputational risk as key reasons for the change. The role will oversee an operational shift roster, while working Monday to Friday as a non-shift worker. Key Responsibilities: Lead, supervise and support Safer Community Officers during rostered shifts. Conduct team briefings and coordinate the appropriate deployment of team members across campus. Oversee the Kalgoorlie Communications Centre, including CCTV, access control, fire alarm and communication systems. Coordinate responses to safety, security, emergency and wellbeing incidents. Provide leadership and direction during emergency and critical incidents. Ensure appropriate escalation and notification processes are followed. Liaise with emergency services and relevant University stakeholders. Respond to wellbeing needs with empathy and provide appropriate support, information and referral pathways. Prepare and maintain accurate incident reports and operational records. Contribute to continuous improvement initiatives and the ongoing development of Safer Community operations. SELECTION CRITERIA Who We Are Looking For: You will be an experienced security professional with the confidence to lead others, make sound decisions under pressure and provide a calm and professional response across a diverse range of operational situations. You will bring: Completion of a Certificate IV or higher in Security Operations through a recognised security training provider, together with extensive relevant experience. Demonstrated ability to lead and supervise a team responsible for customer service and maintaining a safe and secure environment. Strong judgement and the ability to prioritise, initiate action and respond rapidly and appropriately. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, including the ability to work professionally with people experiencing distress, conflict or challenging circumstances. The ability to supervise and work effectively as part of a unified team. Strong written communication and incident reporting skills. Confidence using electronic communication tools, security systems and Microsoft applications. Initiative, resilience and the ability to maintain professionalism under pressure. Role Requirements Current WA Driver’s Licence. Current First Aid Certificate. Mental Health First Aid Training. A level of fitness commensurate with the position. Ability to work outside normal office hours and participate in an operational shift roster. Successful applicants will be subject to a National Police Clearance and background, integrity and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2026 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ Curtin’s Properties Facilities & Development team supports the safe, effective and reliable operation of the University’s physical environment. In Kalgoorlie, the team provides facilities and maintenance support across the campus, student accommodation and Curtin’s expanding residential housing portfolio. Working across everything from laboratories and lecture theatres to accommodation and residential properties, you will be exposed to a variety of facilities and specialised environments, while working closely with university staff, contractors and service providers to keep our facilities safe, functional and well maintained. Curtin Kalgoorlie is located in the heart of Western Australia’s historic Goldfields. Home to the renowned WA School of Mines, the campus has a strong community atmosphere and provides the opportunity to live and work in one of Australia’s most important resources regions. What We Offer You These full-time, permanent positions attract a salary package of $85,781 - $93,836 (G05) plus 17% superannuation, in addition to applicable locality and shift allowances. Make Your Mark: Join Curtin as we establish our in-house Safer Community service in Kalgoorlie and help shape a strong, connected and community-focused operation. Additional Earning Opportunities: Participate in an operational shift roster with applicable shift allowances, with potential overtime opportunities depending on operational requirements. Rewards Beyond Salary: Enjoy 17% superannuation, locality allowance, generous rent subsidy, customisable salary packaging benefits through Paywise, as well as hotel and health insurance discounts. Time Well-Spent: Experience the added value of 17.5% leave loading, with the option to be paid out or taken as additional leave. Benefit from Parental Leave (26 weeks for the primary caregiver), Annual Leave (20 days), Personal Leave (14 days), Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Leave (5 days)*, Travel Leave (2 days), Study Leave and Community Service Leave. Plus 18% more observed days off – enjoy 12 Public Holidays each year, including Easter Tuesday, and two extra University Days over the Christmas/New Year period. Recognition for Prior Service: Curtin recognises prior service for long service leave purposes. New Curtin employees may be* eligible if they have worked for an Australian university, public service or other recognised institution. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join us! Please click APPLY NOW , ensuring you attach the following documents: Cover letter outlining your skills, knowledge and experience relevant to the position. Resume. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our Careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 9.00pm AWST, 29 October 2026 Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=916916": {
      "title": "Research Associate/Fellow",
      "description": "Research Associate/Fellow Faculty of Science & Engineering Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 17/10/2026 Join an internationally connected radio astronomy research environment Grow your research career and contribute to high-impact astrophysics research Enjoy flexible working arrangements, generous super & salary packaging benefits Job Reference: 1424374 About the Role: As a Research Fellow, you will join Professor James Miller-Jones and the CIRA research group, contributing to research focused on high-angular-resolution radio astronomy and jet physics. The group studies accreting compact objects, including X-ray binaries and tidal disruption events, to better understand how these systems launch outflows in the form of relativistic jets. You will work alongside existing team members to apply and further develop novel techniques for modelling high-angular-resolution radio interferometry data from rapidly evolving sources, including X-ray binary jets. This is an excellent opportunity to develop your research profile within a highly collaborative environment while contributing to research at the forefront of radio astronomy and astrophysics. Your responsibilities will include: Contributing to high-quality research outputs and publications. Participating in applications for competitive research funding. Presenting and disseminating research findings through seminars, workshops and conferences. Contributing to the supervision of undergraduate and postgraduate research students. Undertaking research-related administrative activities. Contributing to academic service, engagement and activities across CIRA and the broader University. Who We Are Looking For - Selection Criteria: A doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline (or with no more than ~6 months until completion at the time of interview), such as astronomy, astrophysics, physics, statistics or computer science. Demonstrated knowledge in a relevant discipline and experience with appropriate research techniques. Experience undertaking qualitative and quantitative research tasks. An emerging research track record and demonstrated potential to undertake high-quality research and contribute productively within research teams. Strong organisational, communication and interpersonal skills. A demonstrated commitment to supporting a collegial and inclusive working environment and working effectively with people from diverse backgrounds. Familiarity with time-domain astrophysics, particularly jets from X-ray binary systems. Desirable: Experience working with radio interferometry data, particularly very long baseline interferometry. Experience or skills in statistical modelling and Bayesian inference. Familiarity with X-ray binaries, astronomical jets or time-domain astrophysics. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role. We will also consider international candidates who are eligible for a relevant visa. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction. Your new Team - The Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy (CIRA): CIRA is a multidisciplinary research institute bringing together researchers and professional staff from science, engineering and computing to tackle complex challenges in radio astronomy. CIRA is the lead organisation and operator of the Murchison Widefield Array (MWA) and a key participant in the international Square Kilometre Array (SKA) project. CIRA's research spans a diverse range of projects involving and motivated by radio astronomy, providing an exciting and collaborative environment for researchers seeking to contribute to internationally significant astrophysics research. To learn more about the Curtin Institute of Radio Astronomy and its research, visit: www.astronomy.curtin.edu.au www.icrar.org www.mwatelescope.org www.skao.int Our commitment to a diverse and inclusive workplace: At Curtin University, we are passionate about fostering an inclusive, diverse and welcoming environment in which all people feel respected, valued and safe. Curtin is dedicated to promoting gender equity and inclusivity in all areas. We are proud of our achievements to date and recognise that there is more to do to improve employment outcomes for women and people of diverse gender identities, particularly in STEMM disciplines. As an Athena SWAN accredited institution, we celebrate diversity and actively work to advance the representation of women and gender-diverse people in STEMM areas and empower individuals so they can thrive in their careers through a range of supportive initiatives, including leadership training, mentorship for early career progression and promotion, networking opportunities, and other professional development resources. As reflected in the Athena Swan Charter, gender equality is a broader issue that extends beyond barriers affecting women alone, and we are committed to addressing these challenges holistically. Additionally, we offer staff a competitive remuneration and benefits package, including but not limited to: 17% superannuation, plus a variety of salary packaging options. Paid Parental leave (26 weeks for the primary caregiver and 3 weeks for non-primary) subject to eligibility criteria outlined in the Enterprise Agreement; personal leave (14 days) and annual leave (20 days) Enjoy 12 Public Holidays each year (including Easter Tuesday!), and 2 extra University Days over the Christmas/New Year Period (see EBA 51.1 and 51.9). Breastfeeding facilities and campus-based childcare (where priority of placement is given to Curtin staff and students) Flexible work arrangements including return to work provisions On-campus medical centre Academic Carer Support Scheme and other diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives detailed via the below link. We believe that diverse perspectives fuel innovation and drive excellence, and we encourage women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people of diverse gender, sex and sexuality, people with disability and people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds to join us in shaping the future of STEMM together and making tomorrow better. To find out more about our comprehensive diversity, inclusion and belonging initiatives, please visit https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/values-vision-strategy/diversity-equity/gender-equity-and-inclusion/ About Curtin University Curtin proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This three-year fixed term, full-time ALA/ALB level role attracts a remuneration of $104,165 - $144,352 (ALA.5 - ALB.6) + 17% superannuation . Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and a variety food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and experience A document addressing the selection criteria for the role (noted above) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Monday, 16 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=913050": {
      "title": "Casual Academic",
      "description": "Casual Academic Office of the Provost Perth, Western Australia Casual 7/10/2026 Associate lecturer ALA UniReady Sessional Teaching Pool Based on our vibrant Curtin Bentley Campus Reference: 1693005 About the Role: Join Curtin University's UniReady sessional teaching pool and help transform lives through education. As a Sessional Teaching Academic, you will support students from diverse backgrounds, including equity cohorts and those who may be underrepresented in higher education, to develop the skills, confidence, and academic capability needed for university success. Through engaging teaching, constructive feedback, and a commitment to inclusive practice, you will make a meaningful difference in students' educational journeys while working collaboratively within a supportive and student-focused teaching team. Your responsibilities will include: Deliver engaging, high-quality teaching and learning experiences that support student success in enabling education in any of the following units (Academic Writing, Communications, Numeracy Essentials, Math for Science, Humanities, Commerce, Health Sciences, Science and Technology) Foster inclusive and supportive learning environments that recognise the diverse needs of students and assist their transition to university study. Assess student learning, provide constructive feedback, and support students in achieving intended learning outcomes. Develop and contribute to learning resources, curriculum delivery, and collaborative teaching practices within the UniReady team. Maintain excellence in teaching through innovation, scholarship, professional development, and ongoing engagement with contemporary enabling education practices. Who We Are Looking For/Selection Criteria: Relevant Qualifications and Disciplinary Expertise: A tertiary qualification in a relevant discipline, with demonstrated knowledge and experience appropriate to teaching in an enabling, higher education, adult learning, or senior secondary education context. Student - Centred Teaching and Learning: Demonstrated experience designing, facilitating, or delivering engaging learning experiences that support diverse learners, including students from equity cohorts and educationally underrepresented backgrounds, promote participation, and contribute to positive student outcomes in enabling education. Commitment to Inclusive and Culturally Responsive Practice: Demonstrated ability to create a supportive, inclusive, and culturally responsive learning environment that values diversity and fosters student confidence, belonging, and success. Supporting Academic Development and Student Success: Experience supporting enabling student learners to develop academic skills, self-regulation, confidence, persistence, and independent learning strategies, particularly students transitioning into tertiary education, including the provision of constructive feedback that promotes growth and achievement. Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills, with demonstrated digital literacy and the ability to effectively use learning technologies, work collaboratively within a teaching team, contribute positively to a student-focused culture, and demonstrate a commitment to continuous professional learning and improvement. Remuneration: Remuneration will be in accordance with the Curtin University Casual Academic pay rates, with applicable rates currently ranging from approximately $58.12 per hour (marking) to $174.36 per tutorial delivered, depending on duties performed. Role Requirements: You will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check Your New Team: Centre of Enabling Pathways/UniReady The UniReady team within Curtin University's Centre for Enabling Pathways is committed to helping students from diverse educational and life experiences realise their potential and succeed in higher education. Through supportive, student-centred teaching, our educators foster confidence, academic capability, resilience, and a sense of belonging. We are passionate about creating engaging learning experiences that empower enabling students to transition successfully to university study and achieve their future academic goals. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity please contact Naomi n.jeong@curtin.edu.au on or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Friday 6th of November 2026. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
