    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=922615": {
      "title": "Data Governance & Technical Lead (Project Lead)",
      "description": "Data Governance & Technical Lead (Project Lead) Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 2/11/2026 Full time, 12 month fixed term contract (possibility of extension) Competitive remuneration + 17% super contributions Ranked in the top 1% of universities worldwide (ARWU 2025) Reference Number: 1661338 About the Role: Curtin University is establishing a university-wide secure data research ecosystem to support national and international sensitive-data research collaborations. As the Data Governance & Technical Lead – Secure Research Environments (Project Lead) , you will lead the governance, compliance and accreditation workstream supporting this major initiative. Working closely with the High Performance Computing (HPC) Lead, you will shape Curtin’s secure research environment and ensure it meets institutional, state, Commonwealth and sector accreditation requirements. You will manage project scope, governance, resources, risks and stakeholder expectations to deliver key milestones and outcomes. This is a high-profile opportunity to work across faculties and engage with stakeholders at all levels, bringing together diverse areas of expertise to deliver critical research infrastructure. You will help build Curtin’s capability in secure research and contribute to a project with significant university-wide impact, providing an excellent opportunity to broaden your experience and progress your career. Your responsibilities will include: Lead project planning, coordination and delivery from initiation through implementation and transition to business-as-usual Lead the development of institution-wide governance, compliance, operational, monitoring and accreditation frameworks for Curtin’s secure research and analytics environment Develop data classification, access control and governance models aligned with National Data Scheme (DAT Act) accreditation requirements Lead consultation with faculties, research centres and technical services to identify secure-data research needs and translate these into governance, operational and technical requirements Partner with Curtin’s Indigenous research governance units to embed Aboriginal data sovereignty requirements within the secure research environment Coordinate with government, defence, health and industry partners to co-design bespoke secure analytical environments Manage project scope, risks, resources, timelines and stakeholder expectations to deliver agreed outcomes Who we are looking for (selection criteria): A postgraduate qualification in a relevant discipline with extensive relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education/training High-level planning, project management and leadership skills, with the ability to manage complex projects and deliver outcomes High-level interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to engage, influence and build productive relationships with a diverse range of internal and external stakeholders Sound understanding of governed research access workflows, including ethics approval processes and data access committee operations Experience in data engineering and data management, with software development skills and the ability to work across technical and project delivery environments Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with stakeholders across research, technical and business areas to understand requirements and develop effective solutions Desirable: Knowledge of, and experience in, the tertiary education sector and/or defence, health or government sectors Experience applying Indigenous data governance principles, including the CARE Principles Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check School of Population Health The School of Population Health is dedicated to improving health and wellbeing outcomes for communities both locally and globally. Bringing together expertise across public health, psychology and interdisciplinary health fields, the School is recognised for its collaborative culture, innovative teaching and impactful research. Home to a dynamic community of academics, researchers and industry-connected professionals, the School is committed to addressing some of today’s most significant health challenges - from mental health and emerging diseases to climate change, population growth and health equity. Through its strong focus on education, research and community engagement, the School continues to shape the next generation of health professionals and create meaningful impact across the health sector. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This is a full time, 12 month fixed term contract position (possibility of extension), with a salary range between $142,841 - $150,891 (G09) per annum, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=924915": {
      "title": "Head of School | School of Media, Creative Arts and Social Inquiry",
      "description": "Head of School | School of Media, Creative Arts and Social Inquiry Faculty of Humanities Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 9/11/2026 Full-time, 5-year Head of School appointment $229,829 (ALE) + Head of School allowance + 17% super Lead a high-performing School within Curtin's Faculty of Humanities Job Reference: 1694813 Curtin University is seeking an exceptional and transformational academic leader to become the next Head of School, Media, Creative Arts and Social Inquiry. Curtin University's School of Media, Creative Arts and Social Inquiry (MCASI) brings together a distinctive and diverse academic community spanning the creative arts, media and communication, humanities and social sciences. Combining critical inquiry, creative practice and professional education, the School’s mission is to “imagine and realise better futures through critical thinking and powerful storytelling”. About the Role Reporting to the Pro Vice-Chancellor - Faculty of Humanities, the Head of School will provide strategic, academic and operational leadership to a diverse community of academics, professional staff and students. The successful candidate will lead the School through its next phase of growth and impact, strengthening its disciplinary breadth through responsive and sustainable teaching, research, creative practice, and industry and community partnerships. You will foster a culture of collegiality, creativity and innovation, with a focus on renewal of teaching programmes, expansion of high-quality research, sustainable workforce planning and data-informed, equitable decision-making. As a key member of the Faculty leadership team, you will contribute to Curtin’s broader strategic priorities while strengthening the School’s culture, reputation, influence and partnerships. This role offers the opportunity to: Shape the future direction of a School central to Curtin's vision for Learning Futures. Inspire and develop high-performing culture with academic and professional teams. Enhance the School's research profile and impact. Strengthen relationships with government, industry, professional bodies and community partners. Enable innovation in educational practice, curriculum design and student experience. Contribute to the strategic leadership of one of Australia's leading global universities. About You You are a values‑led and transformational academic leader who brings empathy, sound judgement and purpose to leadership . You bring a strong understanding of contemporary higher education, the evolving education landscape and the opportunities emerging from technological, societal and workforce transformation. With a strong track record of positive impact and working collaboratively, you are committed to empowering others and creating environments where people and ideas thrive. The ideal candidate will have: A doctoral qualification in a related discipline. A distinguished record of academic achievement and transformational leadership, with experience leading high-performing teams in complex environments. A strong record of leading impactful innovation in the Higher Education sector. Strong strategic, financial and operational leadership capability with exceptional stakeholder engagement skills. Well developed skills in establishing and maintaining productive industry partnerships A strong track record of creating and leading management teams operating across diverse portfolios, including research centres. Understanding of contemporary national and international Higher Education sectors including opportunities in regional, rural, and remote communities. Experience in developing productive cross-institutional partnerships A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, inclusion, innovation and continuous improvement. Current substantive appointment as Professor (desirable). For more information about this opportunity, including the position description and selection criteria, please copy and paste the following link into your browser to review the Executive candidate brochure: https://tinyurl.com/HoS-MCASI-Curtin Your Team: School of Media, Creative Arts and Social Inquiry With origins extending to Curtin’s Department of General Studies in 1972, MCASI connects established disciplines with emerging fields of inquiry. Its research and creative practice span culture and technology, digital media, social justice, sustainability, international relations and public policy. The School’s 2025–2030 Research and Creative Practice Strategy prioritises interdisciplinary collaboration, innovative methods, stronger connections between research and teaching, and work with social impact. Collaboration is supported by centres including the Centre for Culture and Technology, Korean Research and Engagement Centre and Centre for Human Rights Education.https://www.curtin.edu.au/about/learning-teaching/humanities/education/ What We Offer You This full-time position attracts a remuneration package of $229,829 (ALE) + 10% Head of School allowance + 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: a current curriculum vitae a cover letter outlining interest in the role and demonstrating how your experience, leadership approach and values align with the responsibilities and capabilities described above. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 11.30PM (AWST), Sunday 6 December 2026 Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=924315": {
      "title": "Fieldwork Officer",
      "description": "Fieldwork Officer Faculty of Health Sciences Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 7/11/2026 2 x Full time, fixed term positions available | Curtin Bentley Campus Generous leave entitlements and a variety of salary packaging options available On-campus amenities including a gym, medical centre, and childcare facilities Reference Number: 1705348 About the Role: Curtin University has an exciting opportunity for two Fieldwork Officers to join our team in full-time, fixed-term capacity at our Bentley Campus. In this role, you will play an important part in supporting students throughout their fieldwork experience, helping to coordinate placements and ensure the process runs smoothly from allocation through to completion. Working closely with academic course and unit coordinators, students and industry partners, you will coordinate the administrative requirements of the Faculty’s fieldwork courses and help resolve day-to-day placement matters. This is a great opportunity for an organised and proactive administrator who enjoys working with a variety of stakeholders in a fast-paced environment. Previous experience using SONIA or similar placement management software, along with database administration experience in a tertiary environment, will be highly regarded. Positions available: Full time, fixed term until 30 June 2027 Full time, fixed term until 31 March 2027 Your responsibilities will include: Review and maintain data in the SONIA placement management software, including extracting and analysing data and compiling reports Manage verbal and written communication with fieldwork industry partners and students before, during and after completion of fieldwork Assist the Fieldwork Coordinator with the preparation, scheduling and coordination of student placements Advise students regarding travel requirements for fieldwork placements Coordinate and organise workshops, training programs and seminars for students and staff and industry fieldwork partners Provide accurate and timely advice/guidance to internal and industry fieldwork partners regarding policies and procedures related to fieldwork placements Who we are looking for (selection criteria): A diploma qualification and considerable relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education/training Demonstrated interpersonal and verbal communication skills with the ability to deal with others sensitively and constructively and to develop and maintain effective professional working relationships with a wide range of stakeholders Well-developed organisational skills with the ability to manage multiple and often competing tasks to meet deadlines Demonstrated written communication skills with the ability to draft a range of correspondence and documentation which is clear and concise Demonstrated ability to use initiative and exercise sound judgment in the resolution of day-to-day issues arising Demonstrated ability to work effectively in a team environment as well as autonomously with limited direction Desirable Criteria: Previous experience in the higher education sector in a student focussed role Previous experience working with SONIA placement system Experience with Student One information system Role Requirements: As this is a continuing (permanent) role, you will require permanent work rights in Australia to be considered Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This full-time, G05 level role attracts a remuneration of $85,781 - $93,836, plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up to date resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) - no more than two pages If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Please note applications will not be accepted via this email address. Applications close: 8:00pm (AWST) on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Late applications or applications received via email will not be considered, and only successful candidates will be contacted. Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. We reserve the right to remove the advert earlier than the date on the advert. Back to search results"
