    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=925844": {
      "title": "Program Coordinators - AHEAD (Multiple Opportunities)",
      "description": "Program Coordinators - AHEAD (Multiple Opportunities) Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 10/11/2026 Are you a teacher or passionate professional looking to make a difference?hhhhhh Three exciting roles delivering innovative programs to improve university access Full-time and part-time fixed-term opportunities with 17% superannuation. Job Reference: 1705855 Are you passionate about education, building meaningful relationships and helping young people discover what's possible for their future? Curtin University is seeking enthusiastic and motivated Program Coordinators to join AHEAD, an innovative educational outreach program dedicated to increasing inclusion and participation in higher education across Western Australia. These opportunities may be particularly appealing to teachers and education professionals looking to apply their classroom experience, creativity and relationship-building skills in a different and rewarding educational setting. About AHEAD AHEAD is a unique educational outreach program that works with schools, students and community partners to help young people explore their interests, discover their potential and understand the many pathways available to higher education. AHEAD takes a university-neutral approach. Our purpose is not to recruit students to any institution, but to empower them with the confidence, knowledge and opportunities to make informed decisions about their educational futures. Through engaging workshops, mentoring, school partnerships and campus experiences, AHEAD supports students who may otherwise face barriers to higher education, particularly those from low socioeconomic and underrepresented backgrounds. By building genuine relationships and creating inclusive learning experiences, we aim to broaden students' aspirations, unlock their interests and demonstrate that higher education can be an achievable and exciting pathway. To learn more about AHEAD and the difference we're making, visit https://pathwaysahead.com.au/. About the Role As a Program Coordinator, you will play an important role in planning, coordinating and delivering innovative educational experiences that inspire young people to explore their future possibilities. Working closely with schools, educators, students and community partners, you will use your creativity, communication skills and ability to build rapport to deliver engaging programs that make higher education more accessible and inclusive. We have three opportunities available, each contributing to AHEAD's broader mission while offering a distinct area of focus. Program Coordinator – Metro Fixed-term, part-time (0.6 FTE) until September 2027 Working closely with metropolitan secondary schools, you will coordinate and deliver engaging educational outreach activities that help students recognise their strengths, explore their interests and understand the pathways available to them beyond school. Key responsibilities include: Planning and delivering interactive, classroom-based workshops and learning experiences in partnership with metropolitan secondary schools. Developing creative and inclusive educational resources that encourage students to explore their interests and future study options. Coordinating student mentors and casual staff to support the delivery of school-based outreach activities. Building strong, positive relationships with teachers, school staff and students to support meaningful engagement. Supporting campus visits and educational experiences that help students build confidence and familiarity with higher education. Program Coordinator – Regional Fixed-term, part-time (0.4 FTE) until December 2027 You will support the delivery of AHEAD across regional Western Australia, creating meaningful educational experiences that respond to the unique needs and aspirations of regional students and communities. Key responsibilities include: Building and maintaining effective partnerships with regional secondary schools, educators and community stakeholders. Planning and delivering engaging educational workshops that inspire students to explore future study and career pathways. Understanding the opportunities and challenges experienced by regional school communities and tailoring learning activities accordingly. Supporting campus visits and educational experiences that increase students' awareness of higher education opportunities. Developing strong relationships with students and school communities to encourage participation, confidence and a sense of belonging. Regional travel within Western Australia will be required. Program Coordinator – Campus & Digital Fixed-term, full-time (1.0 FTE) until December 2027 You will coordinate engaging on-campus and digital learning experiences that introduce students to university life, broaden their aspirations and help them explore the many possibilities available through higher education. Key responsibilities include: Designing, coordinating and delivering interactive educational experiences on university campuses. Developing and supporting engaging digital learning programs and resources for partner schools. Coordinating school visits, casual staff, facilitators and other stakeholders to ensure the successful delivery of program activities. Creating inclusive and welcoming learning environments that build students' confidence and encourage participation. Maintaining an understanding of university courses, entry pathways and educational opportunities across the higher education sector. SELECTION CRITERIA You will be an enthusiastic, proactive and collaborative professional who is passionate about education, student engagement and creating opportunities for young people. Whether your background is in classroom teaching, educational outreach, mentoring, student engagement or program coordination, we are interested in the skills and experience you can bring to AHEAD. You will have a natural ability to build rapport, engage diverse audiences and deliver meaningful learning experiences that inspire curiosity, confidence and participation. To be successful in these opportunities, you will demonstrate: Relevant tertiary qualifications and experience in education, teaching, mentoring, program coordination or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Experience planning, coordinating and delivering engaging educational activities, workshops or learning programs. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to establish positive relationships with students, educators, schools and community stakeholders. Excellent organisational skills, including the ability to manage competing priorities and coordinate multiple activities. Strong written communication skills and the ability to develop educational resources, reports and documentation. A collaborative and adaptable approach, with the ability to work effectively with people from diverse backgrounds. An understanding of educational disadvantage and the barriers that can impact access and participation in higher education. A genuine commitment to inclusion, educational equity and supporting young people to achieve their potential. Depending on the position, additional experience and capabilities may include: Experience teaching or delivering educational activities within primary or secondary school environments. An understanding of the challenges and opportunities experienced by students in regional communities. Knowledge of Western Australia's secondary and tertiary education sectors. Experience developing or delivering digital learning resources and using educational technologies. Applicants will be assessed against the requirements of the specific position(s) for which they are being considered. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2026 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: These fixed-term opportunities are available on a full-time or part-time basis, attracting a salary of $99,213 - $105,251 (G06) (pro rata for part-time appointments) plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If you're passionate about inspiring young people, creating inclusive educational experiences and helping students discover opportunities beyond the classroom, we would love to hear from you. Please click 'APPLY NOW', ensuring to attach the following: Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position's selection criteria (noted above) Up to date Resume As part of the application process, you will be asked to indicate which of the three Program Coordinator opportunities you would like to be considered for. Applicants may nominate more than one position. If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8.00PM (AWST), 24 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=925838": {
      "title": "Transition Support Coordinators",
      "description": "Transition Support Coordinators Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Academic Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 10/11/2026 Two full-time, fixed-term roles until 31 December 2027 | Bentley Campus Enjoy flexible working arrangements including 1 day per week working from home Join a growing team delivering exciting projects in student engagement, orientat Job Reference: 1705855 Starting university is an exciting milestone, but it can also feel overwhelming. The right support, a friendly connection and a sense of belonging can make all the difference. At Curtin, we want every student to feel confident, connected and supported from day one, and you could help make that happen! We have two exciting opportunities for enthusiastic and organised Transition Support Coordinators to join our Student Life and Community team and help bring a new student transition support pilot to life. This is a fantastic opportunity to be part of a growing, collaborative team, contributing to a variety of projects and initiatives that make a meaningful difference to the student experience. About the Role As a Transition Support Coordinator, you'll play a key role in helping students navigate their university journey, particularly those who may benefit from additional support as they transition into higher education. Working within our Student Support team, you'll contribute to a diverse range of year-round initiatives, including Orientation, New to Curtin Mentoring (N2CM), student outreach and transition programs. The team is passionate about creating welcoming, inclusive experiences that help students build confidence, connections and a sense of belonging at Curtin. A key focus of these positions will be coordinating a new transition support pilot, providing targeted outreach to students from low socioeconomic backgrounds, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander students, and those from regional, rural and remote communities. You'll also be able to contribute to new Orientation initiatives, review existing mentoring approaches and develop resources that help students feel informed, prepared and connected. Key Responsibilities: Coordinate and deliver targeted student outreach across semesters, trimesters and Open Universities Australia (OUA) study periods, helping students access the support and services they need to succeed. Build meaningful connections with students and colleagues, providing advice, guidance and appropriate referrals to University support services. Support the delivery and continuous improvement of Orientation programs, including new initiatives designed to create more inclusive and engaging experiences for diverse student cohorts. Contribute to the coordination and enhancement of our New to Curtin Mentoring program, including mentor and mentee recruitment, student engagement and targeted outreach. Develop engaging and accessible student resources, including videos, guides and information materials, to enhance orientation and transition experiences. Monitor student engagement, maintain accurate program records and analyse feedback and trends to identify opportunities for improvement. Collaborate with stakeholders across the University to test new approaches, evaluate outcomes and help shape the future of student support at Curtin. SELECTION CRITERIA You're someone who genuinely enjoys connecting with people and helping others feel welcome and supported. Friendly, approachable and organised, you bring a proactive attitude and enjoy working collaboratively to turn ideas into meaningful experiences. You'll thrive in a role that offers variety, balancing student engagement with the coordination, delivery and evaluation of programs and initiatives. To be successful in this role, you will bring: Demonstrated experience in student services, program coordination or a similar environment, with the ability to support the delivery of engaging and effective programs or initiatives. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build positive relationships and engage confidently with students, colleagues and stakeholders from diverse backgrounds. Strong organisational, planning and time-management skills, with the ability to coordinate multiple activities, maintain accurate records and manage competing priorities. Experience supporting program delivery, student engagement, mentoring or similar initiatives, with the ability to identify opportunities for improvement. Confidence using digital systems, including Microsoft Excel and customer relationship management (CRM) systems, to maintain information, monitor activities and support reporting. A sound understanding of the importance of following relevant policies, procedures and legislation in your work. Experience within higher education, an understanding of student equity and transition challenges, or familiarity with Student One and Open Universities Australia (OUA) would be advantageous, however not essential. Role Requirements As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role. Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin's satisfaction. This role requires that the successful candidate must undergo and maintain an up-to-date Working with Children Check. About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2026 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This full-time, fixed term role attracts a remuneration of $99,213 - $105,251 (G06) per annum plus 17% superannuation. Situated on Whadjuk Nyungar Country, our vibrant Bentley campus includes an art gallery, gym, childcare and medical centre, bus station, accessible parking and endless food options including a supermarket. Aside from the vibrant campus life, we offer more than just a job. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position's selection criteria (noted above) If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST), Tuesday 24 November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    },
    "https://staff.curtin.edu.au/job-vacancies/?ja-job=920383": {
      "title": "Business Manager - Future of Work Institute",
      "description": "Business Manager - Future of Work Institute Faculty of Business & Law Perth, Western Australia Contract Full Time 27/10/2026 Full-Time Fixed term contract Ending 31st December 2027 Diverse & inclusive environment that fosters innovation & collaboration Based In Perth City Campus Reference: 1700248 Full-Time Fixed term contract Ending 31st December 2027 About the Role: The Future of Work Institute (FoWI) helps people and organisations thrive in the digital age through leading-edge research, collaboration and practical impact. The Institute translates research into meaningful outcomes by working with industry, researchers, alumni and community partners on projects that shape the future of work. This role combines business and operational management with strategic support to the Institute. Working closely with the Director and management team, you will help coordinate business planning, resource management, stakeholder engagement, reporting and governance activities that enable FoWI to deliver on its research and impact objectives. You will be a key point of connection between the Institute, Curtin’s internal service areas and external stakeholders, ensuring operations are well coordinated, compliant and aligned with University priorities. In this hands-on role, you will take responsibility for a broad range of activities supporting the Institute’s day-to-day operations. Your responsibilities will include coordinating operational support across the Institute, marketing and communications and organising workshops and events, including those forming part of research project deliverables. You will bring initiative, sound judgement and a practical approach, balancing longer-term priorities with day-to-day delivery and adapting to changing needs. You will be a key point of connection between the Institute, Curtin’s internal service areas and external stakeholders, ensuring operations are well coordinated, compliant and aligned with University priorities. Your responsibilities will include: Contribute as a key member of the Institute’s management team, supporting effective planning, decision-making and operations. Support the Director and management team with business and strategy development, resource planning, allocation of resources and stakeholder management. Act as a key liaison between the Institute, Curtin service areas and relevant internal and external stakeholders to support the Institute’s activities, objectives and day-to-day operations. Work collaboratively with University service groups to develop, implement and improve Institute services, systems and processes. Manage the Institute’s reporting requirements, contractual obligations and operational compliance activities. Provide advice to the Director and Institute staff on operational, regulatory and research-related administrative matters. Support a positive, service-focused team culture that enables efficient delivery of Institute priorities. Selection Criteria: Relevant tertiary qualifications with extensive relevant experience; extensive experience in business management within a complex organisation; or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience in a leadership or management role, including the ability to achieve outcomes through effective planning, coordination and use of resources. Demonstrated experience in business planning, including researching and analysing issues, identifying opportunities for improvement and developing, implementing and evaluating business improvement strategies. Demonstrated team leadership skills, including the ability to build and maintain a customer-focused, collaborative and efficient team culture. Sound knowledge of financial, budget and resource management, including the ability to monitor, report on and support effective use of resources. High-level communication, negotiation and problem-solving skills, including the ability to provide clear advice and resolve stakeholder concerns. High-level interpersonal and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to influence, collaborate and maintain productive relationships with a broad range of internal and external stakeholders. A strong understanding of governance, compliance, reporting or contractual obligations within a research, university or similarly complex environment. Role Requirements: As this is a fixed-term contract, you will require current work rights in Australia that match the tenure of this role Successful applicants are subject to a National Police Clearance, and background, integrity, and reference checks to Curtin’s satisfaction About Curtin University Curtin University proudly stands among the top one percent of global universities according to the ARWU 2025 rankings. We are an energetic, dedicated team of academics and professionals working together to provide global solutions through quality graduates and innovative research. Curtin is a welcoming space where integrity, respect for one another, courage, excellence, and impact are essential to everyday life. First Nations people, those identifying as LGBTQIA+, parents, carers, people of all ages, abilities and genders, and people of diverse ethnicity, nationality and faith are encouraged to apply. Our aim is to create a workforce that reflects the diversity of the community in which we live. For more information, please visit our Diversity and Inclusion page - https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/diversity-equity-and-inclusion-at-curtin/ What We Offer You: This Fixed Term G08 level role attracts a remuneration of $122,699 - $130,758 + 17% superannuation. Curtin offers a wide range of generous benefits, including additional leave, salary packaging, health and wellbeing services and an array of discounts for retail, travel and much more. For more reasons to work at Curtin, please visit https://staff.curtin.edu.au/discover-curtin/ Join Us! If this sounds like your next role, please click APPLY NOW ensuring to attach the following documents: Up-to-date Resume Cover Letter outlining your interest and demonstrating how your background meets the position’s selection criteria (noted above) – 2 – 3 Pages max If you have any queries in relation to this opportunity or the application process, please contact our careers team on CurtinCareers@curtin.edu.au or 08 9266 9000 (option 3,1), quoting the reference number. Applications will not be accepted via this email address, and late applications will not be accepted. Applications close: 8pm (AWST) Tuesday 24th of November 2026 Please note we are not accepting applications from recruitment agencies at this time. Curtin reserves the right at its sole discretion to withdraw from the recruitment process, remove the advert, not to make an appointment, or to appoint by invitation, at any time. Back to search results"
    }
